9. Scale/failure → What breaks?
Now let's construct a production failure.
Suppose:
OrderService
has:
1,000 worker threads
and calls:
PaymentService
PaymentService becomes slow.
Normally:
request duration = 100ms
So each worker is occupied briefly.
But now:
request duration = 30 seconds
If requests keep arriving:
workers
████████████████████████████████
             all occupied
Eventually:
new request
    ↓
no worker
    ↓
queue
    ↓
queue grows
    ↓
latency grows
And now something interesting happens.
The problem started in:
PaymentService
but eventually:
OrderService
also becomes unhealthy.
This is the beginning of a cascading failure.
We'll study this deeply in Lesson 3.
________________________________________
10. Why "just increase the timeout" is dangerous
Suppose your service currently has:
timeout = 2 sec
Some requests fail because PaymentService is slow.
An engineer says:
"Let's increase it to 30 seconds."
Now instead of:
2 sec × many requests
you have:
30 sec × many requests
You've increased the amount of time each request can consume resources.
This can make the system less resilient, not more.
A timeout is therefore not just an error-handling setting.
It is a capacity-control mechanism.
________________________________________
11. Solution → What architecture/component solves it?
A production service generally needs multiple layers of time control.
Conceptually:
                    Request
                       |
                       v
                Overall deadline
                       |
             +---------+---------+
             |                   |
          Connect              Response
          timeout              timeout
For example:
Overall deadline = 3 sec
Connection timeout = 300 ms
Response/read timeout = bounded
The exact numbers depend on the system.
The architecture matters more than the numbers.
________________________________________
12. Internals → What's actually happening?
Let's look underneath HTTP.
At a simplified level:
Application
    |
    v
HTTP client
    |
    v
TCP socket
    |
    v
Network
When you call:
client.send(request);
the client may perform:
1. Resolve host
2. Establish TCP connection
3. TLS handshake
4. Write HTTP request
5. Wait for response
6. Read response
7. Return to application
Timeouts can exist around different stages.
Connection timeout
Controls stage 2.
Read/response timeout
Controls waiting for data during stages 5–6.
Overall request deadline
Controls the entire logical operation.
And here is a subtle but important point:
Timeouts don't stop the remote operation.
Suppose:
OrderService
    |
    | POST payment
    v
PaymentService
    |
    | processes payment
    |
    X
OrderService timeout
OrderService timing out does not mean PaymentService stopped.
PaymentService may continue:
PaymentService
    |
    v
charge card
    |
    v
success
while OrderService has already returned:
timeout
This is exactly the same uncertainty we encountered with Retry.
________________________________________
13. Timeout creates UNKNOWN
This is the connection between our previous topic and this one.
With Retry:
timeout
   ↓
did operation succeed?
   ↓
UNKNOWN
So:
Timeout is one of the major ways a distributed system creates an UNKNOWN outcome.
Example:
OrderService                    PaymentService

   POST /payment -------------------->

                                  charge succeeds

   <------------- response ----------X
                    lost

   timeout
From OrderService's perspective:
UNKNOWN
From PaymentService's perspective:
SUCCESS
Two machines can simultaneously have different realities about the same operation.
That's distributed systems.
________________________________________
14. Trade-offs
Timeouts give us:
Protection
We don't wait forever.
Resource reclamation
Threads/connections can be released.
Failure detection
The caller can move on.
Bounded latency
We can establish an upper bound on waiting.
But we sacrifice:
False failures
The operation might still succeed remotely.
Lost work
We may abandon a request that was close to completion.
Retry complexity
Timeout frequently triggers retry decisions.
Cascading complexity
Poorly chosen timeouts can actually contribute to failure propagation.
________________________________________
15. Java connection
You've probably seen something like:
HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .connectTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(500))
    .build();
That is specifically related to connection establishment.
With other Java HTTP/client libraries you'll encounter concepts named things like:
connectTimeout
readTimeout
responseTimeout
callTimeout
requestTimeout
socketTimeout
deadline
Don't assume these names are interchangeable.
The exact semantics depend on the library.
The useful engineering question is:
"Which phase of the operation does this timer actually bound?"
That's a much better question than:
"What's the timeout value?"
