Lesson 3 — Circuit Breaker: Failure Counting + Sliding Windows
We already established the motivation for circuit breakers:
Service A
   |
   |  HTTP
   v
Service B
Suppose B becomes unhealthy.
Without protection:
A
|
| 1000 requests/sec
v
B  ---> slow ---> timeout
^
|
A retries
|
more load
The interesting problem isn't simply:
“How many requests failed?”
The real question is:
How do we determine, statistically and temporally, that a dependency has become unhealthy enough that continuing to send traffic is harmful?
That takes us directly to failure counting and sliding windows.
________________________________________
1. The naive circuit breaker
Let's start with the simplest possible implementation.
int failureCount = 0;

if (callFails()) {
    failureCount++;
}

if (failureCount >= 5) {
    circuitOpen = true;
}
Looks reasonable.
But it's fundamentally broken.
Imagine:
10:00:00  failure
10:05:00  failure
10:10:00  failure
10:15:00  failure
10:20:00  failure
Eventually:
failureCount = 5
Circuit opens.
But was the dependency actually unhealthy?
Probably not.
Five failures over 20 minutes might be completely normal.
Now consider:
10:20:00.001 failure
10:20:00.002 failure
10:20:00.003 failure
10:20:00.004 failure
10:20:00.005 failure
Five failures within 5 milliseconds.
That's very different.
Therefore:
Failure count without time context is almost meaningless.
We need a window.
________________________________________
2. What is a failure window?
Instead of asking:
“How many failures have ever happened?”
ask:
“How many failures happened recently?”
For example:
Window = last 10 seconds
Failure threshold = 50%
Minimum requests = 20
Suppose the last 10 seconds contain:
20 requests

SUCCESS SUCCESS SUCCESS SUCCESS
FAIL    FAIL    FAIL    FAIL
SUCCESS SUCCESS SUCCESS SUCCESS
FAIL    FAIL    SUCCESS SUCCESS
SUCCESS SUCCESS SUCCESS SUCCESS
That's:
15 success
5 failure

failure rate = 5 / 20
            = 25%
If threshold is 50%:
Circuit remains CLOSED
Now:
20 requests

SUCCESS
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
SUCCESS
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
SUCCESS
17 failures / 20:
85%
Circuit should probably open.
Notice that we're not simply counting failures.
We're calculating:
That distinction becomes extremely important.
________________________________________
3. Why failure percentage is better than failure count
Suppose we configure:
failure threshold = 50%
minimum calls = 20
Consider two situations.
Case A
1000 requests
100 failures
Failure rate:
10%
Probably okay.
Case B
20 requests
15 failures
Failure rate:
75%
Clearly concerning.
But now there is another problem.
What if:
2 requests
2 failures
That's:
100% failure rate
Should the circuit open?
Probably not.
The sample size is too small.
This gives us the first important production rule:
Failure rate should generally be combined with a minimum number of calls.
Conceptually:
if (
    totalCalls >= minimumCalls
    &&
    failureRate >= threshold
) {
    OPEN();
}
For example:
minimumCalls = 20
failureRateThreshold = 50%
Then:
2 calls, 2 failures
doesn't open.
But:
20 calls, 11 failures
might.
________________________________________
4. But now another problem appears
Imagine:
Window = 10 seconds
At:
12:00:00
you have:
20 failures
Circuit opens.
Good.
But then time passes.
At:
12:00:10
those failures shouldn't count anymore.
This is why we need a sliding window.
The word "sliding" is important.
We're continuously asking:
"What happened during the most recent N seconds?"
rather than:
"What happened during some fixed period?"
________________________________________
5. Fixed window vs sliding window
This distinction is important in system design interviews.
Fixed window
Suppose:
window = 10 seconds
You divide time into:
12:00:00 - 12:00:09
12:00:10 - 12:00:19
12:00:20 - 12:00:29
Counters reset at boundaries.
Imagine:
12:00:09.9
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL

12:00:10.1
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
FAIL
You had:
10 failures within 200ms
But because they're on opposite sides of the fixed-window boundary, they may be counted separately.
That's a boundary artifact.
________________________________________
6. Sliding window
A true sliding window doesn't care about artificial clock boundaries.
At:
12:00:10.1
we ask:
What happened from:

12:00:00.1
      to
12:00:10.1
The window moves continuously:
time
------------------------------------------------>

        [-----------]
            window

           [-----------]
               window

              [-----------]
                  window
This gives much more accurate temporal behavior.
But there is a cost.
________________________________________
7. The obvious implementation: store every request
You could maintain:
Deque<CallResult> calls = new ArrayDeque<>();
Each call:
record CallResult(
    long timestamp,
    boolean success
) {}
Then:
void record(CallResult result) {
    calls.addLast(result);

    long cutoff = System.currentTimeMillis() - WINDOW;

    while (!calls.isEmpty()
            && calls.peekFirst().timestamp() < cutoff) {
        calls.removeFirst();
    }
}
Failure rate:
long failures = calls.stream()
        .filter(c -> !c.success())
        .count();

double failureRate =
        (double) failures / calls.size();
Conceptually this works.
But production systems may receive:
100,000 requests/sec
Imagine retaining every call.
For 10 seconds:
1,000,000 events
That's unnecessary.
We don't actually need every event.
We only need aggregate statistics.
This leads to the next important idea.
________________________________________
8. Bucketed sliding windows
Instead of storing every request, divide the window into buckets.
For example:
window = 10 seconds
bucket = 1 second
We maintain:
10 buckets
Each bucket contains:
successfulCalls
failedCalls
slowCalls
Conceptually:
Time

12:00:00   [S=80 F=20]
12:00:01   [S=90 F=10]
12:00:02   [S=95 F=5]
12:00:03   [S=70 F=30]
12:00:04   [S=50 F=50]
12:00:05   [S=40 F=60]
12:00:06   [S=30 F=70]
12:00:07   [S=20 F=80]
12:00:08   [S=10 F=90]
12:00:09   [S=5  F=95]
Total:
success = 490
failure = 510

failureRate = 510 / 1000
            = 51%
Now we can make the decision.
________________________________________
9. Ring buffer
A very natural implementation is a ring buffer.
Imagine:
                 current
                    |
                    v
       +----+----+----+----+----+
       | B0 | B1 | B2 | B3 | B4 |
       +----+----+----+----+----+
          ^                    |
          |____________________|
Suppose we have 10 one-second buckets:
Bucket[] buckets = new Bucket[10];
The current second determines the bucket:
int index = (int) (currentSecond % 10);
When the clock moves to the next second, we reuse the corresponding bucket.
But there's a subtle problem.
Suppose bucket 3 originally represented:
12:00:03
Ten seconds later, we reuse it for:
12:00:13
We must reset it.
Therefore a bucket isn't just:
class Bucket {
    long success;
    long failure;
}
It needs identity:
class Bucket {

    long epochSecond;

    long success;
    long failure;
}
When accessing it:
if (bucket.epochSecond != currentSecond) {
    bucket.epochSecond = currentSecond;
    bucket.success = 0;
    bucket.failure = 0;
}
This prevents stale data from being mixed with new data.
________________________________________
10. Why buckets are powerful
Suppose:
100k requests/sec
10 second window
Event-based approach:
~1,000,000 records
Bucket approach:
10 buckets
The memory requirement becomes effectively:
rather than:
That's a major systems-design principle:
When you need statistics over a time interval, aggregate events into time buckets instead of retaining every event whenever exact per-event information isn't required.
You'll encounter this same idea in:
•	rate limiters 
•	metrics systems 
•	monitoring 
•	traffic shaping 
•	rolling averages 
•	anomaly detection 
•	request-per-second calculations 
________________________________________
11. But there are actually two different window designs
Libraries such as resilience frameworks commonly distinguish between:
Count-based window
Example:
last 100 calls
The circuit breaker evaluates:
failure rate among the last 100 calls
rather than:
calls during last 10 seconds
This answers:
“How bad are the most recent N requests?”
________________________________________
Time-based window
Example:
last 10 seconds
This answers:
“How bad has this dependency been recently?”
These are fundamentally different.
Imagine traffic is:
10 requests/sec
versus:
10,000 requests/sec
A 100-call window represents:
10 seconds
in the first system.
But only:
10 milliseconds
in the second.
Therefore:
Count-based windows normalize by request count; time-based windows normalize by elapsed time.
Neither is universally superior.
________________________________________
12. Let's derive a practical circuit-breaker algorithm
Suppose we choose:
window = 10 seconds
minimumCalls = 20
failureThreshold = 50%
Every call produces one outcome:
SUCCESS
FAILURE
We maintain:
totalCalls
failedCalls
over the window.
Then:
boolean shouldOpen() {

    if (totalCalls < minimumCalls) {
        return false;
    }

    double failureRate =
            (double) failedCalls / totalCalls;

    return failureRate >= 0.50;
}
That's the mathematical core.
But production systems have a much more interesting question:
What exactly is a failure?
________________________________________
13. Failure is not simply "exception"
This is where real circuit-breaker design gets interesting.
Suppose:
try {
    response = client.call();
}
catch (TimeoutException e) {
    // failure
}
Fine.
But what about:
HTTP 400
HTTP 401
HTTP 404
HTTP 409
HTTP 429
HTTP 500
HTTP 502
HTTP 503
HTTP 504
Should all be counted as dependency failures?
No.
Consider:
GET /users/123
returns:
404 Not Found
That might mean the downstream service is perfectly healthy.
The user simply doesn't exist.
Counting 404 as dependency failure would be wrong.
But:
503 Service Unavailable
is much more indicative of dependency health.
Likewise:
Connection timeout
Connection refused
DNS failure
TLS failure
can represent dependency/network failures.
Therefore the circuit breaker needs a failure classification policy.
________________________________________
14. Failure classification
Conceptually:
Outcome classify(Response response) {

    if (response.statusCode() >= 500) {
        return FAILURE;
    }

    if (response.statusCode() == 429) {
        return maybeFailure;
    }

    if (response.statusCode() >= 400) {
        return SUCCESS;
    }

    return SUCCESS;
}
But even this isn't universally correct.
For example:
429 Too Many Requests
might indicate:
our service is sending too much traffic
rather than:
dependency is broken
The classification depends on the architecture.
This is one of the biggest differences between learning circuit breakers and designing one for production.
________________________________________
15. Timeouts are especially important
Imagine downstream latency becomes:
100ms
200ms
500ms
2 sec
5 sec
10 sec
Your application timeout:
2 seconds
So requests begin producing:
TimeoutException
The circuit breaker counts them as failures.
That makes sense.
But now consider a subtle problem.
Suppose your timeout is:
10 seconds
and the circuit breaker timeout is:
2 seconds
The breaker might never get a chance to see the result before its own decision logic becomes relevant.
This is why timeout configuration must be designed as part of the whole call chain.
Think:
client timeout
      |
      v
circuit breaker
      |
      v
retry
      |
      v
load balancer
      |
      v
network
      |
      v
dependency
Not as isolated settings.
________________________________________
16. Slow calls can be failures too
This introduces another dimension.
Suppose:
100 requests
All return HTTP 200.
But:
95 requests take 8 seconds
Technically:
failure rate = 0%
Yet your service may be effectively unusable.
So some circuit breakers track:
failure rate
and also:
slow-call rate
For example:
slow call threshold = 2 seconds
slow call rate threshold = 50%
Then:
100 calls
70 calls > 2 sec
means:
slowCallRate = 70%
The breaker may open even though:
failureRate = 0%
This is a very important production insight:
Availability is not the same thing as responsiveness.
A dependency returning 200 OK after 30 seconds may be operationally equivalent to a failure for your caller.
________________________________________
17. Now let's connect this to the state machine
We have:
CLOSED
OPEN
HALF_OPEN
But now we can understand what each state actually means.
CLOSED
Normal operation.
request
   |
   v
dependency
   |
   v
record outcome
Statistics are continuously collected.
If:
failureRate >= threshold
then:
CLOSED -> OPEN
________________________________________
18. OPEN
No calls should reach the dependency.
Instead:
request
   |
   v
CIRCUIT BREAKER
   |
   +----> reject immediately
This is the critical property.
The circuit breaker is no longer primarily protecting the downstream.
It is protecting your own system from wasting resources on a known-bad dependency.
Rejected calls avoid:
•	connection acquisition 
•	network I/O 
•	thread waiting 
•	socket usage 
•	request queueing 
•	downstream load 
This is why circuit breakers can stop cascading failures.
________________________________________
19. But an OPEN circuit creates another problem
Suppose B was temporarily unhealthy.
You open the circuit:
A ---> X B
If you keep it open forever:
B recovers
but A never notices.
Therefore we need:
OPEN -> HALF_OPEN
after some waiting period.
For example:
waitDuration = 30 seconds
After 30 seconds, we don't immediately allow all traffic.
Instead:
Let's test whether B has recovered.
That's HALF_OPEN.
________________________________________
20. HALF_OPEN is an experiment
This is the most useful way to mentally model it.
Don't think:
HALF_OPEN = another state.
Think:
HALF_OPEN = controlled recovery experiment.
Suppose:
OPEN
Then:
30 seconds pass
Now we allow:
1 probe request
or a small number:
5 requests
Then observe.
Probe succeeds
HALF_OPEN
    |
    | success
    v
 CLOSED
Probe fails
HALF_OPEN
    |
    | failure
    v
 OPEN
________________________________________
21. Why can't we immediately close after one success?
Because one successful request doesn't prove recovery.
Imagine downstream is still unstable:
request 1 -> success
request 2 -> failure
request 3 -> failure
request 4 -> timeout
If we immediately transition:
HALF_OPEN -> CLOSED
after request 1, we've released full traffic into an unhealthy service.
That can recreate the outage.
Therefore production implementations often use a limited number of permitted calls in HALF_OPEN.
For example:
permittedHalfOpenCalls = 5
Then:
probe 1 -> success
probe 2 -> success
probe 3 -> success
probe 4 -> success
probe 5 -> success

=> CLOSED
But:
probe 1 -> success
probe 2 -> failure
may result in:
OPEN
depending on the policy.
________________________________________
22. The thundering herd problem in HALF_OPEN
This is a subtle production issue.
Imagine:
1000 application instances
All have circuit breakers.
Dependency fails.
All instances open their circuits.
Thirty seconds later:
1000 instances
     |
     | HALF_OPEN
     v
dependency
If every instance sends 10 probe requests:
10,000 requests
hit the recovering dependency.
You may accidentally create a second outage.
This is why recovery probing needs to be controlled.
Possible strategies include:
1. limited probes per instance
2. randomized recovery delays
3. jitter
4. centralized coordination
5. adaptive traffic ramp-up
________________________________________
23. Recovery should look like this
Bad:
OPEN
  |
  | wait 30 sec
  v
HALF_OPEN
  |
  | immediately release 100% traffic
  v
CLOSED
Better:
OPEN
  |
  | wait
  v
HALF_OPEN
  |
  | 1 probe
  v
success
  |
  v
more probes
  |
  v
small traffic
  |
  v
normal traffic
This starts to resemble load ramp-up rather than a simple boolean switch.
________________________________________
24. Important concurrency problem
Now let's think like a Java engineer.
Suppose 100 threads simultaneously encounter an OPEN breaker whose recovery timeout has expired.
All execute:
if (now >= nextAttemptTime) {
    state = HALF_OPEN;
}
Without synchronization:
Thread 1 -> sees OPEN
Thread 2 -> sees OPEN
Thread 3 -> sees OPEN
Thread 4 -> sees OPEN

all think:

"I am the probe"
You wanted:
1 probe
You accidentally get:
100 probes
Therefore the state transition:
OPEN -> HALF_OPEN
must be coordinated atomically.
Conceptually:
if (state.compareAndSet(OPEN, HALF_OPEN)) {
    // this thread owns the transition
    // this thread may perform probe
}
Other threads must observe:
HALF_OPEN
and follow the HALF_OPEN admission policy.
This is where the circuit breaker becomes a concurrent state machine, not merely an enum.
________________________________________
25. The state machine as a formal model
Let's define:
S = { CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN }
Events:
failure threshold exceeded
wait timeout elapsed
probe success
probe failure
Transitions:
CLOSED
   |
   | failure rate >= threshold
   v
OPEN
   |
   | wait duration elapsed
   v
HALF_OPEN
   |
   +------ success ------> CLOSED
   |
   +------ failure ------> OPEN
Now add guards.
CLOSED
if totalCalls >= minimumCalls
AND failureRate >= threshold
    -> OPEN
OPEN
if now >= openedAt + waitDuration
    -> HALF_OPEN
HALF_OPEN
if permittedCalls exhausted
AND recovery criteria satisfied
    -> CLOSED
otherwise:
failure
    -> OPEN
That's the actual machine.
________________________________________
26. A subtle question: when exactly do we calculate the window?
Suppose:
window = 10 seconds
At time T:
failure rate = 60%
Breaker opens.
Then we stop recording calls because calls are rejected.
Ten seconds later:
window contains almost no new calls
Should the breaker automatically close because old failures expired?
No.
This is a very important conceptual point.
The circuit breaker shouldn't reason:
“The failures disappeared from the window, therefore the dependency is healthy.”
Why?
Because there were no calls.
No traffic means no evidence of recovery.
That's why OPEN generally uses an independent:
waitDuration
and explicit HALF_OPEN probing.
________________________________________
27. Why this separation matters
There are two different clocks:
Failure observation window
Answers:
How unhealthy has the dependency recently been?
Example:
10 seconds
Recovery wait duration
Answers:
How long should we wait before testing the dependency again?
Example:
30 seconds
They serve different purposes.
Do not conflate:
slidingWindowDuration
with:
waitDurationInOpenState
This is a common configuration mistake.
________________________________________
