29. Let's write a simplified Java implementation
Not production-ready, but useful for understanding the mechanics.
enum State {
    CLOSED,
    OPEN,
    HALF_OPEN
}
Configuration:
record CircuitBreakerConfig(
        int minimumCalls,
        double failureRateThreshold,
        long openDurationMillis,
        int halfOpenProbeLimit
) {}
Statistics:
class Statistics {

    private long total;
    private long failures;

    synchronized void recordSuccess() {
        total++;
    }

    synchronized void recordFailure() {
        total++;
        failures++;
    }

    synchronized double failureRate() {
        if (total == 0) {
            return 0.0;
        }

        return (double) failures / total;
    }

    synchronized long totalCalls() {
        return total;
    }
}
Again: this is educational, not production code.
Now the breaker:
class CircuitBreaker {

    private final CircuitBreakerConfig config;
    private final Statistics statistics = new Statistics();

    private final AtomicReference<State> state =
            new AtomicReference<>(State.CLOSED);

    private volatile long openedAt;

    private final AtomicInteger halfOpenCalls =
            new AtomicInteger();

    CircuitBreaker(CircuitBreakerConfig config) {
        this.config = config;
    }
}
________________________________________
30. Request admission
Every call first passes through:
boolean allowRequest() {

    State current = state.get();

    switch (current) {

        case CLOSED:
            return true;

        case OPEN:
            return tryTransitionToHalfOpen();

        case HALF_OPEN:
            return allowHalfOpenProbe();

        default:
            throw new IllegalStateException();
    }
}
OPEN logic:
boolean tryTransitionToHalfOpen() {

    long now = System.currentTimeMillis();

    if (now - openedAt < config.openDurationMillis()) {
        return false;
    }

    return state.compareAndSet(
            State.OPEN,
            State.HALF_OPEN
    );
}
Only one thread successfully performs:
OPEN -> HALF_OPEN
because of CAS.
But note: this simplistic implementation has a problem.
The thread that successfully changes the state can then make the call, but other concurrent requests may also enter HALF_OPEN.
We therefore need explicit admission control.
________________________________________
31. HALF_OPEN admission control
boolean allowHalfOpenProbe() {

    int current = halfOpenCalls.incrementAndGet();

    if (current <= config.halfOpenProbeLimit()) {
        return true;
    }

    halfOpenCalls.decrementAndGet();

    return false;
}
Now we limit probes.
But again, this has subtle lifecycle issues.
For example:
probe starts
probe hangs
probe never records result
Then the half-open slot remains occupied.
Real implementations must carefully handle:
•	exceptions 
•	timeouts 
•	cancellation 
•	thread interruption 
•	asynchronous completion 
•	duplicate completion 
•	metrics 
________________________________________
32. Recording the result
Conceptually:
void onSuccess() {

    State current = state.get();

    if (current == State.CLOSED) {
        statistics.recordSuccess();
        evaluate();
        return;
    }

    if (current == State.HALF_OPEN) {
        handleHalfOpenSuccess();
    }
}
Failure:
void onFailure(Throwable error) {

    State current = state.get();

    if (current == State.CLOSED) {

        statistics.recordFailure();

        evaluate();

        return;
    }

    if (current == State.HALF_OPEN) {
        state.set(State.OPEN);
        openedAt = System.currentTimeMillis();
    }
}
Again, simplified.
The actual engineering challenge is ensuring every outcome is recorded exactly once.
________________________________________
33. Exactly-once outcome recording
This is a surprisingly important issue.
Imagine:
CompletableFuture<Response> future = call();

future.whenComplete((response, error) -> {
    recordResult();
});
Now someone also catches an exception:
try {
    future.get();
}
catch (Exception e) {
    recordFailure();
}
You might accidentally record:
ONE request
as:
TWO failures
Then the breaker thinks:
failureRate = 80%
when the real rate is:
40%
So a circuit breaker integration must have a clear rule:
One logical call → exactly one terminal outcome.
Possible terminal outcomes:
success
failure
slow-success
slow-failure
ignored
________________________________________
34. What should happen with retries?
This is where architecture gets really interesting.
Suppose:
Application
   |
Circuit Breaker
   |
Retry
   |
Dependency
Application makes:
1 logical request
Retry performs:
attempt 1 -> timeout
attempt 2 -> timeout
attempt 3 -> success
What should circuit breaker count?
Possibility A:
3 failures? 
No, because attempt 3 succeeded.
Possibility B:
1 success
Often this is the desired interpretation if the breaker surrounds the whole retry operation.
But if the breaker is placed inside the retry:
Application
   |
Retry
   |
Circuit Breaker
   |
Dependency
then the breaker may observe:
failure
failure
success
Those are three breaker calls.
This dramatically changes its statistics.
Therefore:
Circuit breaker placement relative to retry changes the meaning of your failure metrics.
________________________________________
35. Retry + Circuit Breaker ordering
Consider:
A
|
Circuit Breaker
|
Retry
|
B
One application request can generate:
1 breaker call
3 downstream attempts
versus:
A
|
Retry
|
Circuit Breaker
|
B
which can generate:
3 breaker calls
These are not equivalent systems.
And there is another danger.
Suppose:
100 requests
each retry three times.
Potential downstream traffic:
300 requests
If B is already failing, retry can amplify the problem.
This is why:
Retries and circuit breakers must be designed together.
________________________________________
36. Failure amplification
Imagine B normally handles:
1000 req/sec
A sends:
800 req/sec
B starts failing.
A retries each request twice.
Now B potentially receives:
800 original
+
1600 retries
=
2400 req/sec
So the dependency is failing partly because the caller is generating additional traffic.
This creates a feedback loop:
failure
  ↓
retry
  ↓
more traffic
  ↓
more overload
  ↓
more failure
  ↓
more retry
Circuit breakers interrupt this loop:
failure
  ↓
failure rate rises
  ↓
OPEN
  ↓
reject calls locally
  ↓
traffic to B drops
That is one of the deepest reasons circuit breakers exist.
________________________________________
37. Another major pitfall: low traffic
Suppose:
minimumCalls = 20
Your service only sends:
2 requests/minute
Dependency is completely dead.
You get:
request 1 -> failure
request 2 -> failure
But:
2 < minimumCalls
so breaker stays closed.
This is not necessarily a bug.
It's a consequence of insufficient statistical evidence.
In low-traffic systems, other mechanisms may be more useful:
timeouts
health checks
synthetic probes
explicit dependency health state
This shows that circuit breakers are not a universal failure-detection mechanism.
________________________________________
38. Another pitfall: high traffic
Now suppose:
1 million requests/sec
and:
minimumCalls = 20
You'll reach the threshold almost instantly.
For example:
20 calls in 20 microseconds
So the breaker might react extremely aggressively.
That may be exactly what you want—or not.
This is why configuration has to be understood relative to:
traffic volume
latency
failure duration
dependency capacity
There is no universally correct:
minimumCalls = 50
or:
window = 10 seconds
________________________________________
39. A better way to choose parameters
Don't start with:
"Resilience4j recommends X"
Start with system behavior.
Ask:
1. What is normal traffic?
RPS = ?
2. What does a meaningful failure burst look like?
For example:
100 failures within 1 second
3. How quickly do we want to stop sending traffic?
reaction time = ?
4. How much evidence do we require?
minimum calls = ?
5. How long should we wait before recovery testing?
open duration = ?
6. How much recovery traffic can B safely handle?
half-open probes = ?
These are architectural questions, not library questions.
________________________________________
40. Sliding window isn't necessarily mathematically exact
Suppose:
10-second window
1-second buckets
At:
12:00:09.999
a bucket may contain requests from almost a full second.
At:
12:00:10.001
we may expire the entire bucket.
Therefore the bucketed approximation can have boundary granularity.
You could increase precision:
100ms buckets
but now:
10 sec / 100ms = 100 buckets
Memory and coordination increase.
This is a classic engineering trade-off:
precision
   vs
memory
   vs
CPU
   vs
concurrency complexity
________________________________________
41. Distributed circuit breakers
Now let's move into system-design territory.
Suppose you have:
A1
A2
A3
A4
A5
Each has its own circuit breaker.
B is failing.
Each instance sees only its own traffic.
Maybe:
A1 -> 50% failure
A2 -> 48%
A3 -> 51%
A4 -> 10%
A5 -> 5%
Some circuits open.
Some stay closed.
Why?
Because the circuit breaker is usually local state.
This is generally desirable.
________________________________________
42. Why not use a shared Redis circuit breaker?
You might think:
A1 \
A2  \
A3 ---> Redis ---> global failure counter
A4  /
A5 /
Then all instances know:
B failure rate = 70%
Sounds better.
But now you've introduced another distributed dependency:
A -> Redis
and potentially:
Redis failure
becomes part of your request path.
You've also introduced:
•	network latency 
•	consistency issues 
•	contention 
•	coordination overhead 
•	Redis availability concerns 
For many applications, local circuit breakers are preferable because:
A circuit breaker is fundamentally a local load-shedding mechanism.
You don't necessarily need global knowledge.
________________________________________
43. Local breaker vs global breaker
Local
A1 ---> breaker ---> B
A2 ---> breaker ---> B
A3 ---> breaker ---> B
Each has independent state.
Advantages:
•	fast 
•	no network coordination 
•	failure isolation 
•	simple 
•	no shared-state dependency 
Disadvantages:
•	inconsistent decisions across instances 
•	each instance needs enough traffic for statistical confidence 
________________________________________
Global
A1 \
A2  \
A3 ---> shared breaker state ---> B
A4  /
A5 /
Advantages:
•	global view 
•	consistent policy 
Disadvantages:
•	coordination 
•	availability dependency 
•	distributed-state complexity 
•	potentially massive contention 
For most ordinary service-to-service calls:
local breaker
is the natural default.
________________________________________
44. Metrics you absolutely want
A production circuit breaker shouldn't be a black box.
At minimum monitor:
circuit_state
calls_total
calls_success
calls_failed
calls_slow
failure_rate
slow_call_rate
not_permitted_calls
