51. Production pitfalls checklist
These are the ones I would expect you to recognize in a senior system-design discussion.
Pitfall 1 — Counting all exceptions as failures
Bad:
catch (Exception e) {
    failure++;
}
Because:
IllegalArgumentException
validation failure
404
business exception
may have nothing to do with dependency health.
________________________________________
Pitfall 2 — No minimum sample size
1 failure
1 request
100% failure
OPEN
Usually too aggressive.
________________________________________
Pitfall 3 — Fixed windows causing boundary effects
10 failures just before boundary
10 failures just after boundary
Can be treated as two separate periods.
________________________________________
Pitfall 4 — Retaining every event
At high RPS:
memory explosion
Use aggregation/buckets when exact event history isn't required.
________________________________________
Pitfall 5 — HALF_OPEN stampede
Thousands of instances recover simultaneously.
Use:
limited probes
jitter
controlled ramp-up
________________________________________
Pitfall 6 — Retry amplification
failure
 -> retry
 -> more failure
 -> retry
Circuit breaker and retry must be designed together.
________________________________________
Pitfall 7 — Ignoring slow calls
0% errors
99% requests take 15 seconds
Your service is still unhealthy.
________________________________________
Pitfall 8 — Treating breaker state as globally authoritative
Different application instances can legitimately have different states.
________________________________________
Pitfall 9 — No observability
If you don't know:
why it opened
when it opened
how many calls it rejected
incident debugging becomes painful.
________________________________________
Pitfall 10 — Bad fallback
A fallback that violates business correctness can be worse than returning an error.
