31. Bulkhead design
There are two common conceptual forms.
Semaphore bulkhead
Limit concurrent operations:
maxConcurrentCalls = 50
Conceptually:
Semaphore semaphore = new Semaphore(50);
Before calling:
semaphore.acquire();
After completion:
semaphore.release();
If no slot is available:
reject
or potentially:
wait
depending on design.
________________________________________
32. Why waiting for a bulkhead slot can be dangerous
Suppose:
maxConcurrent = 50
and 500 requests arrive.
If 450 simply wait:
450 threads blocked
You've limited downstream concurrency but may still exhaust upstream resources.
Therefore a bulkhead should usually be paired with a bounded wait or immediate rejection, depending on the workload.
For latency-sensitive APIs:
no slot
→ fail fast
is often better than:
wait indefinitely
________________________________________
33. Thread-pool bulkhead
Another approach is a dedicated thread pool:
Order Service
    |
    +--> Payment thread pool
    |      20 threads
    |
    +--> Inventory thread pool
           20 threads
Now Payment becoming slow cannot consume all application worker threads.
This is stronger isolation.
But it has costs:
•	extra threads 
•	context switching 
•	queue management 
•	memory 
•	tuning complexity 
Modern asynchronous/reactive systems may use different isolation mechanisms.
________________________________________
34. Why bulkheads are called bulkheads
Think of a ship:
+---------+---------+---------+
| Engine  | Payment | Search  |
| room    | room    | room    |
+---------+---------+---------+
If Payment compartment floods:
+---------+---------+---------+
| Engine  | FLOODED | Search  |
|         | XXXXXXX |         |
+---------+---------+---------+
the other compartments remain operational.
In software:
Payment dependency
shouldn't be able to consume:
all threads
all connections
all queues
all CPU
needed by unrelated operations.
That's the core of bulkheading.
________________________________________
35. Bulkhead by dependency
Suppose:
Service A
 |
 +---- Payment
 |
 +---- Inventory
 |
 +---- Recommendation
 |
 +---- Email
Bad:
one shared resource pool
Payment becomes slow:
Payment → consumes everything
Better:
Payment       → 50 concurrent
Inventory     → 50 concurrent
Recommendation→ 20 concurrent
Email         → 10 concurrent
Now failures are isolated.
This is resource partitioning by failure domain.
That's a very important system-design concept.
