________________________________________
23. But don't over-isolate blindly
Here's the senior-level warning.
Bulkheads introduce complexity.
Too many pools can cause:
configuration explosion
monitoring complexity
underutilization
thread explosion
memory overhead
difficult capacity planning
So don't create:
ThreadPool1
ThreadPool2
ThreadPool3
ThreadPool4
...
ThreadPool27
just because "Bulkhead is good."
Instead:
Identify workloads that have different failure characteristics, priorities, or resource requirements.
Then isolate those workloads.
Good candidates often include:
critical vs non-critical operations
slow vs fast dependencies
untrusted/high-volume workloads
different downstream services
different resource classes
________________________________________
24. A very important distinction: Bulkhead doesn't fix overload
This is subtle.
Suppose:
Payment capacity = 20
Traffic = 1000 req/sec
Payment can process = 50 req/sec
Bulkhead doesn't make Payment process 1000 requests/sec.
It says:
"Payment can consume only this much of our system's resources."
The excess work still needs a strategy:
reject
queue briefly
fallback
shed load
rate limit upstream
So:
Bulkhead contains overload; it doesn't eliminate overload.
That's an important interview distinction.
________________________________________
25. Bulkhead vs Rate Limiter
Another common confusion.
Rate limiter
Controls:
requests per unit of time
Example:
100 requests/sec
Concurrency limit / Bulkhead
Controls:
requests simultaneously in progress
Example:
20 concurrent requests
These are different.
Suppose:
100 req/sec
latency = 5 sec
Then:
concurrency ≈ 500
You could have a rate limit of 100/sec but still have 500 in-flight requests.
A concurrency limit might say:
max 20 in-flight
Both can be useful.
________________________________________
