Lesson 1 — Why Rate Limiting Exists
Let's start before algorithms.
Suppose you have:
Client
   |
   v
API Gateway
   |
   v
Order Service
   |
   +----> PostgreSQL
   |
   +----> Payment Service
   |
   +----> Kafka
Your API exposes:
POST /orders
Suppose the service can safely process:
2,000 requests/sec
But a client sends:
50,000 requests/sec
What happens?
The obvious answer is:
"The service becomes slow."
But that's only the beginning.
________________________________________
1. Rate limiting is really a resource-protection mechanism
Think about your system as a collection of finite resources.
For example:
CPU
Memory
DB connections
DB IOPS
Network bandwidth
Kafka partitions
Thread pools
External API quota
Connection pools
Locks
Every request consumes some amount of those resources.
Conceptually:
request
   |
   +--> CPU
   +--> memory
   +--> DB connection
   +--> DB query
   +--> network
   +--> downstream API
Therefore:
Rate limiting controls how quickly work is allowed to enter a system.
That's much more important than the common definition:
"Allow only N requests per second."
The latter is merely one implementation.
________________________________________
2. Why can't we just autoscale?
This is an important system-design question.
Suppose traffic increases:
1000 req/s
       ↓
5000 req/s
       ↓
20000 req/s
You might say:
"Kubernetes will add more pods."
True—but autoscaling doesn't solve every problem.
Imagine:
             20,000 req/s
                  |
        +---------+---------+
        |         |         |
       Pod       Pod       Pod
        |         |         |
        +---------+---------+
                  |
                DB
             5,000 QPS
Your application layer may scale horizontally, but your database may not.
You have simply moved the bottleneck:
Application capacity: 20k req/s
Database capacity:     5k req/s
The database becomes the system bottleneck.
Rate limiting allows us to enforce:
Incoming traffic <= sustainable capacity
________________________________________
3. Rate limiting protects against two very different things
This distinction is extremely important.
A. Accidental overload
Example:
A mobile application has a bug.
Instead of:
1 request / 10 seconds
it accidentally sends:
100 requests/sec
Nothing malicious is happening.
Rate limiting protects your infrastructure anyway.
________________________________________
B. Intentional abuse
Example:
Attacker
   |
   +---- 100k requests/sec
   |
   v
Your API
Potential goals:
•	brute-force login 
•	scrape data 
•	exhaust expensive APIs 
•	create excessive DB load 
•	consume compute 
•	cause denial of service 
Rate limiting becomes one layer of defense.
But remember:
Rate limiting is not a complete DDoS defense.
At sufficiently large traffic volumes, traffic may need to be stopped at CDN, WAF, load-balancer, network, or upstream infrastructure before it reaches your application.
________________________________________
4. Rate limiting also protects downstream systems
This is where experienced backend engineers should think beyond the API itself.
Imagine:
API
 |
 v
Order Service
 |
 v
Payment Service
Your service can process:
10,000 req/s
But payment provider allows:
1,000 req/s
Without limiting:
10,000 req/s
       |
       v
Payment API
       |
       X
   throttling
Your application may then experience:
timeouts
retries
thread exhaustion
connection exhaustion
queue growth
latency explosion
And now connect this to what you've already learned.
You completed:
Timeout
Retry
Circuit Breaker
Bulkhead
Rate limiting is closely related.
For example:
Rate Limiter
     |
     v
protect request admission
     |
     v
Timeout
     |
     v
Retry
     |
     v
Circuit Breaker
     |
     v
Bulkhead
But these solve different failure dimensions.
________________________________________
5. Rate limiting vs timeout vs circuit breaker vs bulkhead
This distinction is worth mastering.
Mechanism	Main question
Rate limiting	How much work may enter?
Timeout	How long may one operation take?
Retry	Should failed work be attempted again?
Circuit breaker	Should we temporarily stop calling an unhealthy dependency?
Bulkhead	How much concurrent work may consume this resource?
For example:
Rate limit
100 requests/sec
Controls rate.
Bulkhead
maximum 50 concurrent requests
Controls concurrency.
Timeout
maximum 2 seconds/request
Controls duration.
These dimensions are different.
________________________________________
6. Rate vs concurrency
This is one of the most important concepts.
Suppose:
Rate = 100 requests/sec
Does that mean there can only be 100 requests executing simultaneously?
No.
Imagine each request takes 10 seconds.
You could have approximately:
100 req/sec × 10 sec
= 1,000 concurrent requests
This relationship is roughly described by Little's Law:
L = λW
where:
L = average number of requests in system
λ = arrival rate
W = average time in system
Example:
λ = 100 requests/sec
W = 2 sec

L = 100 × 2
  = 200 concurrent requests
This is why rate limiting and bulkheading are complementary.
You might have:
Rate limiter:
1000 req/sec

Bulkhead:
100 concurrent requests

Timeout:
2 seconds
Each protects a different dimension.
________________________________________
7. Rate limiting provides admission control
This is a powerful way to think about it.
Imagine a nightclub.
Capacity:
500 people
Outside:
10,000 people
You don't allow everyone inside and then discover the building is overloaded.
You control admission.
                  +----------------+
10k people -----> | Admission      |
                  | control        |
                  +-------+--------+
                          |
                     500 allowed
                          |
                          v
                       Club
Rate limiting does something similar:
                  +----------------+
Requests -------->| Rate Limiter  |
                  +-------+--------+
                          |
                  accepted / rejected
                          |
                          v
                       Service
This is called:
Admission control
And this concept appears throughout distributed systems.
________________________________________
8. But "100 requests/sec" isn't enough
Now we reach the interesting part.
Suppose the rule is:
Maximum = 100 requests/minute
A naïve implementation might say:
00:00 → 100 requests allowed
00:01 → counter reset
Consider this traffic:
00:00:59
    100 requests

00:01:00
    100 requests
You've technically respected:
100 requests per minute
for each fixed window.
But during one real 2-second period:
200 requests
were accepted.
This leads us to our first algorithm.
________________________________________
Lesson 2 — Fixed Window
The basic idea is extremely simple.
For each client:
counter = 0
window = 60 seconds
Requests:
request
   |
   v
counter++
   |
   +---- counter <= limit ? accept
   |
   +---- counter > limit ? reject
Suppose:
limit = 100 requests/minute
For client:
user-123
we maintain:
window: 10:00 - 10:01
count: 73
Request arrives:
count = 74
Accept.
Eventually:
count = 100
Next request:
101 > 100
Reject.
At 10:01:
counter = 0
and a new window starts.
________________________________________
9. Why Fixed Window is attractive
It's extremely cheap.
You basically need:
clientId -> counter
For example:
user-123 -> 73
user-456 -> 12
user-789 -> 99
And expiration:
user-123 -> expires in 43 sec
In Redis this maps naturally to:
INCR key
EXPIRE key
Conceptually:
INCR rate:user-123
If result:
<= 100
accept.
Otherwise:
reject
This makes fixed-window limiting:
•	simple 
•	fast 
•	memory efficient 
•	easy to distribute 
But it has a serious weakness.
________________________________________
10. The boundary problem
Suppose:
Limit = 100 requests/minute
Traffic:
10:00:59 → 100 requests
10:01:00 → 100 requests
The algorithm says:
Window 1:
100 <= 100 → accept

Window 2:
100 <= 100 → accept
So:
200 requests
can happen within roughly:
1 second
This is the fixed-window boundary burst problem.
Visually:
                 window boundary
                       |
                       v
       100 requests    |    100 requests
    <----------------->|<----------------->
       window 1         |      window 2
That's why fixed window gives you a somewhat misleading guarantee.
It doesn't really mean:
"At most 100 requests in every arbitrary 60-second interval."
It means:
"At most 100 requests inside each predefined 60-second bucket."
Those are not equivalent.
________________________________________
11. This distinction matters in system design
When someone says:
"Our API allows 100 requests per minute."
Your next question should be:
"What exactly does that guarantee mean?"
Possible interpretations:
Fixed calendar window
10:00:00 → 10:00:59
10:01:00 → 10:01:59
Rolling 60 seconds
For every request at time t:
[t - 60 sec, t]
contains at most 100 requests.
Token bucket
Something different again:
steady refill rate
+
controlled burst capacity
These produce different behaviors.
________________________________________
12. Fixed window's deeper trade-off
Why would anyone still use it?
Because distributed systems are about trade-offs, not finding the theoretically perfect algorithm.
Suppose you have:
10 million clients
A sophisticated sliding-window implementation may require significantly more state.
Fixed window might only require:
one counter/client/window
That's attractive.
If your requirement is:
"Prevent obvious abuse; exact boundary behavior doesn't matter."
Fixed window can be perfectly reasonable.
But if your requirement is:
"Strictly control traffic over arbitrary rolling intervals."
You need something more sophisticated.

Lesson 2 — Sliding Window Rate Limiting
Good. Now let's move from the weakness of Fixed Window to the two main sliding-window approaches:
Fixed Window
     ↓
Sliding Window Log
     ↓
Sliding Window Counter
The important thing is not memorizing these algorithms. You should understand what guarantee each one is trying to provide, what state it needs, and what it costs.
________________________________________
1. First: what does "sliding window" actually mean?
Suppose our rule is:
100 requests / 60 seconds
A fixed window asks:
"How many requests occurred inside this predefined bucket?"
For example:
10:00:00 ───────────────── 10:01:00
              100 requests
Then:
10:01:00 ───────────────── 10:02:00
              100 requests
The problem is that the boundary is artificial.
A sliding window instead asks:
For the current request at time t, how many requests occurred during the previous 60 seconds?
So if the current time is:
10:00:37
we examine:
09:59:37 ───────────────── 10:00:37
At:
10:00:38
the window becomes:
09:59:38 ───────────────── 10:00:38
The window literally slides forward with time.
________________________________________
2. Why this fixes the boundary problem
Remember our fixed-window attack:
Limit = 100/minute

10:00:59 → 100 requests
10:01:00 → 100 requests
Fixed window says:
Window A = 100 ✓
Window B = 100 ✓
But sliding window sees:
10:00:00
       |
       |       200 requests
       |<------------------->|
       10:00:59       10:01:00
If those 200 requests occurred within the previous 60 seconds, the second batch cannot simply reset the counter.
The system says:
previous 60 sec = 200
limit = 100

200 > 100
Therefore:
REJECT
That's the fundamental advantage.
________________________________________
3. Sliding Window Log
Let's start with the mathematically clean version.
For every client, maintain the timestamp of every accepted request.
Suppose:
limit = 5 requests / 10 seconds
Client:
user-123
Request timestamps:
[10.0, 11.2, 13.5, 15.1, 18.7]
Now a request arrives at:
20.0
We look back 10 seconds:
[10.0, 11.2, 13.5, 15.1, 18.7]
  ^

10.0 is exactly 10 seconds old
Depending on your interval convention, you might remove timestamps at or before the lower boundary.
For a half-open interval:
(t - window, t]
we remove:
10.0
Remaining:
[11.2, 13.5, 15.1, 18.7]
Count:
4
Therefore the new request is allowed:
[11.2, 13.5, 15.1, 18.7, 20.0]
Now there are 5 requests in the current sliding window.
________________________________________
4. The algorithm
Conceptually:
request arrives
      |
      v
currentTime = now()
      |
      v
remove timestamps <= currentTime - window
      |
      v
count remaining timestamps
      |
      +---- count >= limit ---> REJECT
      |
      +---- count < limit ----> append timestamp
                                  |
                                  v
                                ACCEPT
The data structure is basically:
clientId -> queue of timestamps
For example:
user-123 -> [11.2, 13.5, 15.1, 18.7]
user-456 -> [12.1, 16.8]
user-789 -> [19.3]
Because timestamps arrive in chronological order, a queue/deque is natural.
________________________________________
5. Why a queue works
This is an important implementation observation.
Suppose:
window = 60 sec
and the queue contains:
[100, 105, 110, 120, 150]
Current time:
160
Oldest allowed boundary:
160 - 60 = 100
Remove expired timestamps from the front.
[100, 105, 110, 120, 150]
  ↑
expired
After removal:
[105, 110, 120, 150]
We never need to search the middle.
That gives efficient cleanup assuming timestamps are inserted in order.
________________________________________
6. The big problem: memory
Now let's think like a system designer.
Suppose:
limit = 1,000 requests/sec
and you have:
10 million clients
Worst-case state could approach:
10 million × 1,000 timestamps
That's:
10 billion timestamps
Obviously unacceptable.
Even if the average traffic is much lower, storing individual timestamps is expensive.
This is the first major lesson:
Sliding Window Log gives excellent accuracy by storing detailed history, but detailed history costs memory.
________________________________________
7. Complexity
For one client:
Let:
N = number of requests inside the window
Then state is approximately:
O(N)
per client.
Across all clients:
O(total requests in active windows)
That's fundamentally different from fixed window.
Fixed window might need:
client → integer
Sliding log needs:
client → collection of timestamps
So:
Fixed Window
Memory: O(clients)

Sliding Log
Memory: O(requests in active windows)
This difference becomes huge at scale.
________________________________________
8. There's another problem: distributed concurrency
Suppose you implement:
if queue.size() < limit:
    queue.add(now)
    accept
Two threads arrive simultaneously.
Current state:
count = 99
limit = 100
Thread A:
reads 99
Thread B:
reads 99
Both conclude:
99 < 100
Both append.
Now:
count = 101
You've violated the limit.
This is a classic check-then-act race condition.
So the operation needs to behave atomically:
remove expired
+
count
+
decide
+
insert timestamp
must effectively happen as one atomic operation.
This becomes particularly important when we later implement distributed rate limiting with Redis.
________________________________________
9. Sliding Window Log in Redis
A natural Redis structure is a sorted set.
Conceptually:
key = rate:user-123
Members:
request-id-1
request-id-2
request-id-3
Scores:
1710000000123
1710000000456
1710000000789
The score is the timestamp.
Then the algorithm becomes roughly:
1. Remove entries older than window
2. Count remaining entries
3. If count >= limit → reject
4. Otherwise add current request
Redis has the right primitive for this kind of timestamp ordering.
But there is a critical issue:
Steps 1–4 cannot safely be implemented as independent network calls.
Otherwise:
Client A
   |
   | remove
   | count
   | add
   v

Client B
   |
   | remove
   | count
   | add
can interleave.
We eventually need an atomic server-side operation, commonly using Lua scripting or an equivalent atomic mechanism.
We'll cover this deeply in the Redis lesson.
________________________________________
10. So why don't we always use Sliding Window Log?
Because it's accurate but expensive.
Imagine an API:
GET /search
with:
10,000,000 users
and:
100 requests/minute/user
A log-based limiter potentially stores millions/billions of individual timestamps.
But notice something interesting.
Do we actually need to know:
exactly which 100 requests occurred?
Usually not.
We mostly need:
approximately how many requests occurred recently?
That observation leads to:
Sliding Window Counter
________________________________________
11. Sliding Window Counter
Instead of storing every request timestamp, we divide time into smaller fixed buckets.
Suppose:
window = 60 seconds
We divide it into:
6 × 10-second buckets
Instead of:
timestamp timestamp timestamp timestamp ...
we maintain:
10:00:00 → 13
10:00:10 → 18
10:00:20 → 7
10:00:30 → 22
10:00:40 → 11
10:00:50 → 19
Total:
90 requests
Much less state.
________________________________________
12. But how do we slide the window?
Suppose current time is:
10:01:05
The last 60 seconds are approximately:
10:00:05 → 10:01:05
Our buckets are:
10:00:00 - 10:00:10
10:00:10 - 10:00:20
...
10:00:50 - 10:01:00
10:01:00 - 10:01:10
The current sliding window cuts through two buckets:
             sliding window
             <------------>

10:00:05 ───────────────── 10:01:05
     |                         |
     v                         v
  bucket                    current
  partially                partially
  included                  included
So we need to estimate the partial contribution of those buckets.
________________________________________
13. The interpolation idea
Suppose:
previous bucket count = 100
current bucket count  = 20
and the current point is 25% into the current bucket.
Then approximately 75% of the previous bucket belongs to the sliding window.
So:
estimated previous contribution
≈ 100 × 0.75
= 75
Then:
estimated sliding count
≈ 75 + 20
= 95
If:
limit = 100
the request can be accepted.
This is the key idea behind the common sliding-window counter approximation.
________________________________________
14. Why is it only an approximation?
Because we're not storing individual request timestamps.
Suppose the previous bucket contains:
100 requests
We don't know whether those 100 requests happened:
at the beginning
or:
at the end
of that bucket.
We only know:
bucket count = 100
Therefore interpolation estimates how many belong to the actual sliding interval.
This gives us an important spectrum:
                  Accuracy
                     ↑
                     |
Sliding Log     ██████████
                     |
Sliding Counter ███████
                     |
Fixed Window    █████
                     |
                     +------------→ memory / complexity
The exact position depends on implementation, bucket size, workload, and required semantics, but the conceptual trade-off is what matters.
________________________________________
15. Bucket size becomes a design parameter
Suppose:
window = 60 sec
Option A:
6 buckets × 10 sec
Option B:
60 buckets × 1 sec
Option C:
2 buckets × 30 sec
Generally:
smaller buckets
      ↓
better temporal resolution
      ↓
better approximation
      ↓
more state
while:
larger buckets
      ↓
less state
      ↓
coarser approximation
So this isn't merely an algorithm choice.
It's a capacity-vs-accuracy engineering decision.
________________________________________
16. Compare the three algorithms
Now put them together.
Property	Fixed Window	Sliding Log	Sliding Counter
State	Counter	Every timestamp	Bucket counters
Accuracy	Low	Very high	Approximate
Memory	Very low	High	Low/medium
Boundary burst	Yes	No	Reduced
Implementation	Simple	More complex	Moderate
Distributed implementation	Easy-ish	More expensive	Moderate
Good for huge scale	Yes	Potentially expensive	Yes
Exact rolling-window semantics	No	Yes	Approximate
The crucial mental model is:
Fixed Window
    ↓
"I only know the count in this bucket."

Sliding Log
    ↓
"I know every request."

Sliding Counter
    ↓
"I know counts for smaller buckets."
________________________________________
17. A deeper distributed-systems observation
There is something subtle here.
Rate limiting isn't simply:
counter + if
It is a state consistency problem.
You have state:
number/timestamps of recent requests
and multiple application instances:
             Load Balancer
                  |
       +----------+----------+
       |          |          |
     Pod A      Pod B      Pod C
       |          |          |
       +----------+----------+
                  |
               Limiter
If every pod maintains:
local counter
then your global limit isn't really global.
Suppose:
limit = 100 req/sec
and:
10 pods
Each pod independently allows:
100 req/sec
You could accidentally allow:
10 × 100
= 1,000 req/sec
So distributed rate limiting requires shared state or coordinated partitioning.
That's why Redis appears so often in real architectures.
________________________________________
18. But Redis introduces another question
Suppose Redis is unavailable.
Your request path becomes:
Client
  |
  v
API
  |
  v
Redis Rate Limiter
  |
  X Redis unavailable
Now what?
You have to decide:
Fail open
Redis unavailable
      ↓
allow request
Availability wins, protection weakens.
Fail closed
Redis unavailable
      ↓
reject request
Protection wins, availability suffers.
There isn't a universally correct answer.
For:
public API
you may choose one policy.
For:
expensive payment operation
you may choose another.
For:
security-sensitive endpoint
you may choose another again.
This is why production rate limiting is much more interesting than the algorithm itself.
________________________________________
19. What I want you to remember from this lesson
Don't memorize:
"Sliding window uses timestamps."
Instead remember this progression:
Fixed Window
asks:
How many requests are in this predefined bucket?
↓
Sliding Window Log
asks:
What exact requests occurred during the last W seconds?
↓
Sliding Window Counter
asks:
Can I approximate that answer using aggregated time buckets?
And the engineering trade-off is:
More information
      ↓
better accuracy
      ↓
more memory/state
      ↓
more distributed-system complexity
________________________________________
20. One important interview question
If an interviewer asks:
"Design a rate limiter for 1 million users, 100 requests/minute/user."
Don't immediately answer:
"I'll use Redis."
First ask:
1.	Is the limit per user, per API key, per IP, or global? 
2.	Is the limit a hard guarantee or approximate? 
3.	Are bursts allowed? 
4.	What happens when the limiter is unavailable? 
5.	Do we need a rolling-window guarantee? 
6.	What's the acceptable memory cost? 
7.	What's the expected request rate? 
8.	How many application instances exist? 
9.	Does the limit need to be globally consistent? 
10.	What is the acceptable false-rejection/false-acceptance behavior? 
Those questions determine the algorithm.

Lesson 3 — Token Bucket
This is the rate-limiting algorithm I want you to understand mathematically, because Token Bucket is one of the most useful models in real production systems.
The key idea is surprisingly simple:
A request doesn't consume "permission per second." It consumes a token. Tokens accumulate over time, up to a maximum capacity.
That gives us something Fixed Window and Sliding Window don't naturally provide:
controlled bursts + controlled long-term rate.
________________________________________
1. Start with the problem
Suppose our API should support:
10 requests / second
A naïve interpretation is:
every second → allow exactly 10
But real traffic isn't perfectly smooth.
Imagine a user loading a page.
The browser might simultaneously request:
GET /profile
GET /orders
GET /notifications
GET /recommendations
GET /preferences
Five requests arrive almost simultaneously.
You don't necessarily want to reject them just because they happened within the same millisecond.
What you really want is something like:
Long-term average:
10 requests/sec

Short burst:
perhaps 20 requests immediately
Then the system should return to approximately:
10 requests/sec
That is exactly what Token Bucket models.
________________________________________
2. The physical analogy
Imagine a bucket.
             tokens enter
                  ↓
             +---------+
             | • • • • |
             | • • • • |
             | • • • • |
             +---------+
                  |
                  ↓
              requests
There are three parameters:
Bucket capacity
Maximum number of tokens:
C = 20 tokens
Refill rate
Tokens added per second:
R = 10 tokens/sec
Request cost
Usually:
1 request = 1 token
So:
capacity = 20
refill rate = 10/sec
cost = 1
________________________________________
3. What happens when requests arrive?
Initially:
tokens = 20
A request arrives.
Consume one:
20 → 19
Another:
19 → 18
After 5 requests:
20 → 15
Nothing complicated yet.
________________________________________
4. The interesting part: refill
Suppose we've consumed all tokens:
tokens = 0
The refill process adds:
10 tokens/sec
After:
100 ms
we have approximately:
1 token
After:
500 ms
we have:
5 tokens
After:
1 second
we have:
10 tokens
After:
2 seconds
we would have:
20 tokens
but the bucket's capacity is only 20.
So:
tokens = min(capacity, currentTokens + refill)
This min() is extremely important.
Tokens cannot accumulate forever.
________________________________________
5. The mathematical model
Let's define:
C = bucket capacity
r = refill rate, tokens/sec
T = current time
t_last = last time bucket was updated
Elapsed time:
Δt = T - t_last
New token count:
tokens = min(
    C,
    tokens + Δt × r
)
Then the request costs:
cost = 1
If:
tokens >= cost
accept:
tokens = tokens - cost
Otherwise:
reject
That's essentially the entire algorithm.
But the consequences are much more interesting.
________________________________________
6. Why 10 req/sec can allow 20 requests instantly
This is one of the most common interview questions.
Configuration:
C = 20
r = 10 tokens/sec
Bucket starts full:
20 tokens
Now 20 requests arrive simultaneously:
R1 → token 20 → 19
R2 → 19 → 18
...
R20 → 1 → 0
All 20 are accepted.
You might ask:
"But the limit is 10 requests/sec!"
No.
The configuration actually means:
Maximum burst = 20 requests
Long-term replenishment = 10 requests/sec
This distinction is fundamental.
________________________________________
7. Burst capacity vs sustained rate
Think of Token Bucket as having two independent controls.
Refill rate
Controls:
How much traffic can the system sustain over time?
r = 10 requests/sec
Bucket capacity
Controls:
How large of a burst can the system absorb?
C = 20 requests
So:
              Token Bucket
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |                 |
     refill rate       capacity
          |                 |
     sustained rate     burst size
This is one reason Token Bucket is so powerful.
________________________________________
8. Visualizing traffic
Suppose:
r = 10/sec
C = 20
Traffic can look like:
requests
  ^
20| ████████████████████
  |                     \
10|                      █████ █████ █████
  | 
  +--------------------------------------> time
       burst             sustained
The bucket lets you borrow against previously accumulated capacity.
If the system has been quiet for a while, tokens accumulate.
Then the client can spend those accumulated tokens in a burst.
________________________________________
9. Think of tokens as stored permission
This mental model is useful.
A token is effectively:
permission to perform one unit of work.
When traffic is low:
requests < refill rate
tokens accumulate.
When traffic suddenly increases:
requests > refill rate
the client spends those stored permissions.
Eventually:
tokens → 0
and now the sustained traffic is constrained by the refill rate.
So Token Bucket naturally transforms:
quiet period
     ↓
stored capacity
     ↓
burst
     ↓
empty bucket
     ↓
sustained rate
________________________________________
10. Compare with Fixed Window
Suppose:
10 requests/sec
Fixed Window:
00.0 ───────── 01.0
       10
Then:
01.0 ───────── 02.0
       10
Boundary problem:
00.99 → 10 requests
01.01 → 10 requests
Potentially:
20 requests in ~20 ms
Token Bucket doesn't have that artificial reset boundary.
Tokens refill continuously according to elapsed time.
________________________________________
11. Compare with Sliding Window
Sliding Window tries to answer:
How many requests happened in the previous W seconds?
Token Bucket asks something different:
How much permission has accumulated to perform work?
That's a very important distinction.
For example:
Sliding Window:
100 requests / 60 seconds
is about a rolling historical count.
Token Bucket:
capacity = 100
refill = 100/60 sec
is about accumulated credit.
These can produce different behavior even if their parameters look similar.
________________________________________
12. Token Bucket supports weighted requests
This is where it becomes particularly useful for APIs.
Not every request costs the same.
Suppose:
GET /health
cost = 1

GET /profile
cost = 2

POST /generate-report
cost = 20
Now your bucket isn't really limiting:
requests
It is limiting:
work units
Suppose:
tokens = 50
A report request:
cost = 20
leaves:
50 - 20 = 30
Another report:
30 - 20 = 10
Another:
10 < 20
Reject.
This lets you model heterogeneous workloads.
________________________________________
13. Why weighted rate limiting is powerful
Imagine your service has three endpoints:
GET /users
GET /orders
POST /analytics/report
Their average resource consumption:
users:
1 CPU unit

orders:
3 CPU units

report:
20 CPU units
A simple request-count limiter treats them equally:
1 request = 1
That's not necessarily what you want.
A weighted token bucket can approximate:
users   → 1 token
orders  → 3 tokens
report  → 20 tokens
Now you're limiting resource consumption, not merely HTTP request count.
This is a much more advanced way of thinking about rate limiting.
________________________________________
14. Fractional refill and time
In real implementations, don't think:
every second:
    add 10 tokens
That is a possible implementation, but it's often unnecessary.
Instead use lazy refill.
Suppose:
r = 10 tokens/sec
At:
t = 0
tokens = 5
No background job runs.
Request arrives at:
t = 0.7 sec
Calculate:
elapsed = 0.7
refill = 0.7 × 10
       = 7
Therefore:
tokens = min(C, 5 + 7)
       = 12
Then consume one:
12 → 11
This is much more efficient.
No timer is required per user.
________________________________________
15. This is a beautiful optimization
Imagine:
10 million users
If you have a background refill task for every user:
user 1 → timer
user 2 → timer
user 3 → timer
...
user 10M → timer
That's ridiculous.
Lazy refill means:
We calculate what the bucket should contain only when a request arrives.
State can simply be:
clientId
tokens
lastUpdated
For example:
user-123:
    tokens = 13.4
    lastUpdated = 17:55:42.300
That's enough to reconstruct the current bucket state.
________________________________________
16. Java implementation thinking
A conceptual Java model:
class Bucket {
    double tokens;
    long lastRefillNanos;
}
On request:
long now = System.nanoTime();

long elapsed = now - bucket.lastRefillNanos;

double refill =
        elapsed / 1_000_000_000.0 * refillRate;

bucket.tokens =
        Math.min(capacity, bucket.tokens + refill);

bucket.lastRefillNanos = now;

if (bucket.tokens >= cost) {
    bucket.tokens -= cost;
    return ALLOW;
}

return REJECT;
This is conceptual, not production-ready.
Why?
Because concurrency immediately becomes a problem.
________________________________________
17. Concurrency problem
Suppose:
tokens = 1
Two threads arrive simultaneously.
Thread A             Thread B
   |                    |
read tokens = 1      read tokens = 1
   |                    |
1 >= 1 ✓              1 >= 1 ✓
   |                    |
consume               consume
Both may succeed.
But only one token existed.
You've overspent the bucket.
So the following state transition must be atomic:
refill
+
check
+
consume
+
update timestamp
This is a read-modify-write atomicity problem.
In a single JVM, you could use:
synchronized
or locks / atomic state structures.
But in a distributed system:
Pod A
Pod B
Pod C
a JVM lock isn't enough.
That's where distributed rate limiting becomes interesting.
________________________________________
18. Distributed Token Bucket
Imagine:
                  Load Balancer
                       |
          +------------+------------+
          |            |            |
        Pod A        Pod B        Pod C
          |            |            |
          +------------+------------+
                       |
                     Redis
Suppose global bucket state is:
user-123:
    tokens = 7.5
    lastRefill = T
All pods must coordinate around this shared state.
The operation conceptually becomes:
1. Read current tokens
2. Calculate elapsed time
3. Refill
4. Cap at capacity
5. Check cost
6. Consume if possible
7. Persist tokens + timestamp
Those operations must be atomic.
Otherwise:
Pod A ─┐
       ├── race
Pod B ─┘
can overspend tokens.
________________________________________
19. Why Redis is a natural fit
Redis gives us:
•	low latency 
•	centralized shared state 
•	atomic operations 
•	Lua scripting 
•	TTL 
•	sorted sets / hashes / strings 
•	horizontal deployment options 
For a token bucket, we often need only small state such as:
tokens
last_refill_timestamp
which is much cheaper than a sliding-window log containing every request timestamp.
This is one reason Token Bucket is attractive at high scale.
________________________________________
20. Token Bucket vs Leaky Bucket
You will often hear:
Token Bucket
Leaky Bucket
Don't treat them as synonyms.
A useful conceptual distinction:
Token Bucket
Allows controlled bursts.
        burst
          ↓
████████████████
          \
           \ steady traffic
Leaky Bucket
Conceptually processes traffic at a controlled output rate.
incoming
   ↓
queue
   ↓
████████████
   ↓
constant output
So:
Token Bucket → burst tolerance
Leaky Bucket → smoother output
There are different implementations and terminology in networking literature, but this conceptual distinction is extremely useful for system design.
________________________________________
21. A subtle question: what happens after idle time?
Suppose:
capacity = 100
rate = 10/sec
The client makes no requests for:
1 hour
Do we accumulate:
10 × 3600 = 36,000 tokens?
No.
The bucket is capped:
tokens <= 100
So after enough idle time:
tokens = 100
This matters because otherwise an idle client could return after an hour and generate a gigantic burst.
The bucket capacity is therefore a maximum stored credit.
________________________________________
22. Deriving maximum burst
Suppose:
C = 100
r = 10/sec
Maximum instantaneous burst from a full bucket:
B = C
So:
B = 100 requests
After that, tokens arrive at:
10/sec
If the client continuously consumes at:
20 requests/sec
then:
net depletion = 20 - 10
              = 10 tokens/sec
Starting with 100 tokens:
time to empty = 100 / 10
              = 10 seconds
This gives you a useful capacity calculation.
________________________________________
23. Sustainable rate
If requests cost one token:
sustainable request rate ≈ refill rate
So:
r = 10 tokens/sec
means long-term sustainable traffic is approximately:
10 requests/sec
assuming the bucket is not repeatedly allowed to refill above that consumption rate.
The bucket capacity determines how much temporary excess traffic can be absorbed.
________________________________________
24. A useful formula
If:
C = bucket capacity
r = refill rate
λ = incoming request rate
and:
λ > r
the bucket is draining at approximately:
λ - r
tokens/sec.
Therefore, if initially full:
time to exhaustion ≈ C / (λ - r)
Example:
C = 100
r = 10
λ = 25
Then:
time ≈ 100 / (25 - 10)
     ≈ 6.67 sec
So the system can temporarily absorb 25 req/sec, but after roughly 6.67 seconds the bucket is exhausted.
That's the sort of reasoning that makes you strong in system design.
________________________________________
25. Token Bucket as a traffic envelope
There's a deeper mathematical interpretation.
For a bucket with:
capacity = C
rate = r
over a sufficiently long interval of length t, the amount of traffic allowed is bounded approximately by:
A(t) <= C + r × t
where:
C = initial burst credit
r × t = newly generated credit
This is a powerful way to understand the algorithm.
For example:
C = 100
r = 10/sec
During a 5-second interval, the maximum theoretical amount that can pass, starting with a full bucket, is approximately:
100 + (10 × 5)
= 150
The exact interpretation depends on the initial bucket state and request timing, but the traffic envelope is:
burst allowance + sustained allowance
That is much more precise than saying:
"10 requests per second."
________________________________________
26. Token Bucket gives you a two-dimensional SLA
This is perhaps the most useful mental model.
Instead of:
10 req/sec
you can specify:
Sustained rate = 10 req/sec
Burst capacity = 100 requests
So your API contract becomes:
              burst capacity
                    |
                    v
            +---------------+
            |      100      |
            +---------------+
                   /
                  /
                 /  10/sec
                /
---------------/------------------> time
This is much closer to how real production traffic behaves.
________________________________________
27. Where Token Bucket fits in your overall system
Imagine:
                    Internet
                       |
                       v
                 CDN / WAF
                       |
                       v
                API Gateway
                       |
                Rate Limiter
                       |
              +--------+--------+
              |                 |
         Authentication      Routing
              |                 |
              +--------+--------+
                       |
                 Application
                       |
             +---------+---------+
             |         |         |
            DB       Kafka    External API
You may have multiple token buckets.
For example:
Global:
10,000 req/sec

Per API key:
100 req/sec

Per endpoint:
50 req/sec

Per expensive operation:
