We'll eventually discuss how these limits interact.
________________________________________
28. One subtle production issue: fairness
Suppose you have:
100 requests/sec global
and two users:
User A → 99 req/sec
User B → 1 req/sec
The global limiter is perfectly satisfied.
But is the system fair?
Maybe not.
User A can dominate the shared capacity.
This is why production systems often combine:
Global limit
+
Per-user limit
+
Per-IP limit
+
Endpoint limit
For example:
Global       → 10,000/sec
Per customer → 100/sec
Per IP        → 50/sec
Expensive API → 5/sec
Now we're moving from rate limiting algorithms into fairness and resource allocation, which is one of the final topics in your roadmap.
________________________________________
29. Token Bucket vs Sliding Window: the key distinction
If you remember only one thing from this lesson, remember this:
Sliding Window
Controls recent historical activity:
"How many requests happened recently?"
Token Bucket
Controls accumulated permission:
"How much work is currently allowed?"
Therefore:
Sliding Window
      ↓
history-oriented

Token Bucket
      ↓
credit-oriented
This difference explains why Token Bucket naturally handles bursts.
________________________________________
30. Interview-level answer
If an interviewer asks:
"Why would you choose Token Bucket?"
A strong answer is:
"I'd use Token Bucket when I want to enforce a sustainable average rate while allowing bounded bursts. The refill rate controls long-term throughput, while bucket capacity controls the maximum accumulated burst. It can be implemented with small per-client state—typically current token count and last-refill timestamp—and is therefore attractive for distributed rate limiting. I'd still need to make the refill/check/consume operation atomic across instances, and I'd define a failure policy for when the shared limiter is unavailable."
That's a much stronger answer than:
"Token Bucket allows N requests per second."

Lesson 4 — Distributed Rate Limiting with Redis
Now we move from algorithm design to distributed-systems design.
This is where the real engineering begins.
You already understand Token Bucket:
capacity
refill rate
tokens
consume
The question now is:
How do multiple Java instances enforce one shared rate limit correctly?
________________________________________
1. The problem: multiple application instances
Suppose you have:
                    Load Balancer
                         |
          +--------------+--------------+
          |              |              |
       Java Pod A     Java Pod B     Java Pod C
          |              |              |
          +--------------+--------------+
                         |
                      Database
Your API receives:
1000 requests/sec
You want:
user-123 → maximum 100 requests/sec
The first instinct might be to put a token bucket inside every JVM.
Pod A:
user-123 → 100 tokens

Pod B:
user-123 → 100 tokens

Pod C:
user-123 → 100 tokens
But now your actual limit is approximately:
100 × 3 = 300 requests/sec
instead of:
100 requests/sec
You've created three independent limiters.
________________________________________
2. Why local memory doesn't give a global limit
Imagine four pods:
              user-123
                  |
           Load Balancer
                  |
       +----------+----------+
       |          |          |
      A          B          C
Suppose each JVM says:
maximum = 100/sec
Traffic:
A → 100
B → 100
C → 100
Total:
300/sec
The application thinks:
"I enforced 100/sec."
But the system actually allowed:
300/sec
This is the first fundamental distributed-rate-limiting principle:
If the limit is global, the limiter's state must be globally coordinated.
________________________________________
3. Possible solutions
There are several architectural approaches.
Approach A — Sticky routing
Route the same user consistently to the same pod.
user-123 → Pod A
user-456 → Pod B
user-789 → Pod C
Then local state could work.
But this introduces problems:
•	uneven load 
•	pod failures 
•	rebalancing complexity 
•	scaling complications 
•	hot users 
•	more complicated load balancing 
So it is rarely the preferred general solution.
________________________________________
Approach B — Centralized limiter
                 +-----------+
Request --------> Redis      |
                 | limiter   |
                 +-----------+
                       |
                       v
                    allow?
                       |
                       v
                    Service
All application instances consult the same shared state.
This is the common architecture.
________________________________________
Approach C — Dedicated rate-limit service
You could build:
Java Pods
    |
    v
Rate Limit Service
    |
    v
Redis
This is useful when rate limiting becomes a platform capability shared by many services.
But now you've introduced another network hop and another distributed service.
For many systems, having the application communicate directly with Redis is simpler.
________________________________________
4. Why Redis?
Redis is a natural fit because the limiter needs:
very low latency
shared state
atomic operations
high throughput
expiration
And Redis provides primitives useful for this.
For Token Bucket, the state can be tiny.
For example:
rate:user-123
    tokens = 37
    last_refill = 1723740000123
You don't need to store every request.
Compare that with Sliding Window Log:
rate:user-123
    1723740000001
    1723740000014
    1723740000032
    1723740000055
    ...
Token Bucket is therefore particularly attractive for large-scale distributed limiting.
________________________________________
5. The Redis data model
For a Token Bucket, conceptually we need:
key
 ├── tokens
 └── lastRefillTime
For example:
rate_limit:user-123

tokens = 37.5
lastRefill = 1723740000123
Configuration might be:
capacity = 100
refillRate = 10 tokens/sec
When a request arrives:
currentTime = now
Calculate:
elapsed = currentTime - lastRefill
Then:
newTokens =
    min(capacity,
        tokens + elapsed × refillRate)
Then:
if newTokens >= requestCost:
    newTokens -= requestCost
    ALLOW
else:
    REJECT
Finally store:
tokens = newTokens
lastRefill = currentTime
That is the state transition.
________________________________________
6. But here's the critical problem
You might implement this in Java:
GET tokens
GET lastRefill

calculate refill

if enough tokens:
    SET tokens
    SET lastRefill
Looks reasonable.
But it's incorrect under concurrency.
Suppose Redis contains:
tokens = 1
Two requests arrive:
Pod A                 Pod B
  |                     |
  | GET tokens          |
  |-------------------->|
  |       1             |
  |                     |
  |                     | GET tokens
  |                     |------------>
  |                     |      1
Both see:
tokens = 1
Both say:
1 >= 1
Both accept.
Now you've spent:
2 tokens
when only one existed.
________________________________________
7. This is a classic atomicity problem
The operation isn't simply:
GET
or:
SET
The operation is:
READ
   ↓
CALCULATE
   ↓
CHECK
   ↓
CONSUME
   ↓
WRITE
That entire sequence must behave atomically.
Think of it as:
              +----------------------+
request ----> | Token Bucket         |
              |                      |
              | refill               |
              | check                |
              | consume              |
              | update               |
              +----------------------+
No other request should be able to interleave inside that state transition.
This is exactly where Redis Lua scripting becomes useful.
________________________________________
8. Redis Lua: move the decision to Redis
Instead of:
Java
 |
 +--> GET
 |
 +--> calculate
 |
 +--> SET
we send the algorithm to Redis:
Java
 |
 | execute script
 v
Redis
 |
 +-- read state
 +-- calculate refill
 +-- check tokens
 +-- consume
 +-- update state
 |
 v
ALLOW / REJECT
The important property is:
The script executes atomically within Redis.
Conceptually:
-- pseudo-code

read tokens
read lastRefill

calculate elapsed
calculate refill

tokens = min(capacity, tokens + refill)

if tokens >= cost then
    tokens = tokens - cost
    save tokens
    save timestamp
    return ALLOW
else
    save state
    return REJECT
end
This prevents the race we saw earlier.
________________________________________
9. Why "atomic" matters so much
Suppose:
tokens = 1
Requests A and B arrive.
With atomic execution:
Redis

Request A:
    read 1
    consume
    write 0
    return ALLOW

Request B:
    read 0
    reject
Correct.
Without atomicity:
A reads 1
B reads 1

A accepts
B accepts

tokens = 0
Incorrect.
The final value looks reasonable:
tokens = 0
but two requests were admitted.
This is an important distributed-systems lesson:
Correct final state does not necessarily mean correct concurrent behavior.
________________________________________
10. Don't use Redis INCR blindly
A common beginner implementation is:
INCR rate:user-123
and:
if count <= limit
    allow
This works reasonably for a simple Fixed Window limiter.
But Token Bucket is different.
We need:
tokens
+
time elapsed
+
refill
+
capacity
+
cost
A single integer counter cannot represent that state.
You need at least:
tokens
lastRefill
So:
INCR
alone isn't enough.
________________________________________
11. Fixed Window with Redis
For comparison, Fixed Window is much simpler.
Suppose:
100 requests/minute
Key:
rate:user-123:2026-08-15T18:05
Then conceptually:
INCR key
EXPIRE key 60
If:
count <= 100
allow.
This is why Fixed Window is so popular:
algorithm
    ↓
counter
    ↓
Redis INCR
Very simple.
Token Bucket:
algorithm
    ↓
state + time calculation
    ↓
atomic read/modify/write
More sophisticated.
________________________________________
12. TTL and state cleanup
Suppose a user makes one request.
You create:
rate_limit:user-123
What happens if the user disappears forever?
You don't want Redis accumulating:
millions of inactive users
So rate-limiter keys should generally have expiration.
But there's a subtlety.
For Token Bucket, expiration isn't simply:
EXPIRE = 60 seconds
because the bucket state represents something different from a fixed window.
You typically want to expire state after some period of inactivity.
For example:
last request
     |
     +------ inactivity ------+
                              |
                              v
                         delete state
When the user returns:
new bucket = full bucket
or whatever initialization policy your system defines.
________________________________________
13. Initialization matters
Suppose a brand-new user appears.
Should they receive:
tokens = capacity
?
Usually, yes.
Example:
capacity = 100
First request:
new bucket
tokens = 100
consume:
99
This allows a new client to burst immediately.
But that's a policy decision.
You could initialize differently if your business requirement demands it.
The important thing is:
Define the initial state explicitly.
________________________________________
14. Redis latency is now part of your API latency
Our request path has become:
Client
   |
   v
Load Balancer
   |
   v
Java
   |
   v
Redis
   |
   v
Java
   |
   v
Business logic
Every request now depends on Redis.
Suppose:
Application processing = 10 ms
Redis = 1 ms
Fine.
But if Redis becomes:
50 ms
your API latency increases significantly.
And if Redis is unavailable:
?
Now we reach a very important production question.
________________________________________
15. What happens when Redis fails?
There are two classic strategies.
Fail open
Redis unavailable
       |
       v
ALLOW request
Advantages:
•	application remains available 
•	user requests aren't blocked by limiter outage 
Disadvantages:
•	rate limit protection disappears 
•	downstream systems can become overloaded 
•	abusive traffic can get through 
________________________________________
Fail closed
Redis unavailable
       |
       v
REJECT request
Advantages:
•	protects infrastructure 
•	protects downstream dependencies 
•	preserves the safety boundary 
Disadvantages:
•	legitimate traffic fails 
•	Redis becomes effectively part of your availability boundary 
Neither is universally correct.
________________________________________
16. A better production answer: classify the endpoint
Suppose:
GET /products
and:
POST /transfer-money
Should they have identical failure behavior?
Probably not.
For a low-risk read API:
Redis failure → potentially fail open
For an expensive or security-sensitive operation:
Redis failure → potentially fail closed
You can also use:
local emergency limiter
as a fallback.
Architecture:
                  Redis
                    |
              available?
              /         \
            yes          no
            |             |
         global        local
         limiter       limiter
The fallback isn't equivalent to the global limiter, but it can prevent total loss of protection.
________________________________________
17. Local + distributed limiter
A sophisticated production architecture can have:
Request
   |
   v
Local limiter
   |
   v
Redis limiter
   |
   v
Application
Why local first?
Suppose an obviously abusive client sends:
100,000 req/sec
You don't want every request to reach Redis just to learn:
REJECT
A local limiter can cheaply reject some traffic.
Then Redis enforces the distributed/global policy.
Conceptually:
                 Request
                    |
                    v
             Local limiter
              /          \
          reject         allow
            |              |
          return           v
                        Redis
                     /          \
                 reject        allow
                    |             |
                  return          app
This can reduce Redis load substantially.
But it introduces a new problem:
Local limits must be designed carefully so they don't unfairly reject traffic that should be globally allowed.
________________________________________
18. Redis Cluster and the hot-key problem
Now imagine:
user-123
is an extremely active customer.
All requests hit:
rate_limit:user-123
That's one Redis key.
In Redis Cluster, a key maps to a particular hash slot.
Therefore:
10,000 requests/sec
        |
        v
rate_limit:user-123
        |
        v
one shard
That key can become a hot key.
Your overall Redis cluster might have:
10 shards
but one customer could overload one shard.
This is an important scalability problem.
________________________________________
19. Why can't we just split the key?
You might think:
user-123-1
user-123-2
user-123-3
and distribute them across shards.
But now the limiter needs to know the global token state across multiple shards.
You've traded:
hot key
for:
distributed coordination
which is considerably harder.
This leads to an important principle:
A globally precise limiter inherently requires some form of coordination.
At very large scale, you often have to decide how much precision you're willing to sacrifice to gain scalability.
________________________________________
20. The "exact global limit" problem
Suppose:
limit = 1000 req/sec
and you have:
100 application instances
Could we simply give each instance:
10 req/sec
?
Not really.
Suppose traffic distribution is:
Pod A → 500 req/sec
Pod B → 20
Pod C → 5
...
Pod A needs more capacity while others have unused capacity.
Static allocation is inefficient.
You could dynamically redistribute quotas, but now you've built a coordination protocol.
This is why centralized or coordinated limiting is attractive when precise global semantics matter.
________________________________________
21. Multi-region rate limiting
Now let's make the system harder.
Suppose:
                 Global DNS
                /          \
               /            \
        US Region         EU Region
           |                  |
        Redis US           Redis EU
Global limit:
user-123 → 100 requests/sec
If both regions independently allow:
US → 100/sec
EU → 100/sec
global traffic can become:
200/sec
So what do we do?
________________________________________
Option A — Single global Redis
US ────────┐
           |
EU ────────+──> Global Redis
           |
APAC ──────┘
Strong global coordination.
But:
•	cross-region latency 
•	network dependency 
•	global outage blast radius 
•	harder availability characteristics 
________________________________________
Option B — Regional quotas
For example:
Global = 100/sec

US  = 50/sec
EU  = 30/sec
APAC = 20/sec
Now each region can enforce locally.
Much lower latency.
But traffic isn't always distributed according to those assumptions.
You may need quota redistribution.
________________________________________
Option C — Approximate global limiting
Allow some controlled deviation.
For example:
target = 100/sec
actual = occasionally 105–110/sec
If the business can tolerate this, the system becomes much easier to scale.
This is a fundamental distributed-systems trade-off:
more consistency
       ↕
more coordination
       ↕
more latency / complexity
versus:
more availability / scalability
       ↕
less precise global enforcement
________________________________________
22. Time is another subtle problem
Token Bucket depends on:
elapsed time
So time becomes part of correctness.
Imagine:
Pod A clock = 12:00:00.100
Pod B clock = 11:59:59.900
If each instance independently calculates elapsed time, clock skew can produce incorrect refill calculations.
A distributed limiter should therefore carefully decide where authoritative time comes from.
Possible approaches include:
•	Redis/server-side time 
•	carefully controlled monotonic time locally 
•	centralized timestamp generation 
•	accepting bounded clock skew 
A Java detail:
System.currentTimeMillis()
is wall-clock time.
It can move forward or backward due to clock adjustments.
For measuring elapsed durations within one JVM:
System.nanoTime()
is generally the better primitive because it is monotonic.
But you cannot directly compare nanoTime() values across different machines.
That's an important distinction.
________________________________________
23. Redis server time can help
If the limiter's state lives in Redis, you can have the atomic operation obtain time from the Redis side rather than trusting each Java pod's wall clock.
Conceptually:
Java
  |
  | execute atomic limiter script
  v
Redis
  |
  +--> get current server time
  |
  +--> calculate refill
  |
  +--> consume tokens
  |
  +--> return result
Now the calculation has a single time authority for that limiter operation.
That can simplify correctness.
________________________________________
24. What should the Redis response contain?
Don't just return:
true / false
A useful rate limiter can return:
allowed
remaining
retryAfter
For example:
allowed = false
remaining = 0
retryAfter = 250ms
Then the API can produce:
HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Retry-After: 1
and potentially rate-limit headers such as:
Limit
Remaining
Reset
The exact header contract should be designed consistently across your API.
________________________________________
25. Why Retry-After is important
Suppose the user is rejected:
tokens = 0
but:
refillRate = 10 tokens/sec
and the request costs:
1 token
Then approximately:
time until next token
≈ 1 / 10
≈ 100ms
Returning a retry hint is much better than forcing the client to blindly retry.
And now connect this to your previous lesson.
If the client does:
429
  ↓
immediately retry
  ↓
429
  ↓
retry
  ↓
429
you can create a retry storm.
This is why rate limiting and your previously studied retry/backoff mechanisms are connected.
________________________________________
26. The dangerous interaction: Retry + Rate Limiting
Imagine:
100 clients
all hit the rate limit.
All receive:
429
Suppose every client retries after exactly:
1 second
Then:
t=0       100 requests → rejected
          |
          ↓
t=1 sec   100 requests → retry simultaneously
          |
          ↓
          burst
You've created synchronized traffic.
Better clients use:
exponential backoff
+
jitter
So the retries spread out:
client A → 0.82s
client B → 1.13s
client C → 1.47s
client D → 0.91s
...
This is an excellent example of how different resilience patterns interact.
________________________________________
27. Production architecture
A reasonable architecture might look like:
                         Internet
                            |
                            v
