35. What you should be able to explain now
At this point, if I ask:
"Why can't I implement a global rate limiter using an AtomicLong in each Java pod?"
You should answer:
Because each pod has independent state. An atomic operation provides thread safety only within that JVM; it doesn't coordinate state across instances. A global limit requires shared or coordinated state.
If I ask:
"Why isn't Redis GET + SET enough?"
You should answer:
Because the limiter operation is a read-modify-write transaction. Concurrent requests can read the same token balance and both consume it. The refill, check, consume, and update operations need atomic execution, such as a Redis Lua script.
If I ask:
"Why Token Bucket rather than Sliding Window?"
You should answer:
Token Bucket naturally provides a sustainable rate plus bounded burst capacity, while requiring relatively small state. Sliding Window is more directly concerned with the number of requests in a rolling historical interval and can require substantially more state.
If I ask:
"What happens if Redis goes down?"
The correct senior-level answer isn't simply:
"Fail open."
You should say:
It depends on the protected resource and availability requirements. We need an explicit failure policy—fail-open, fail-closed, or a local fallback limiter—and should consider the security, overload, and availability consequences.
That's the level of reasoning I want you to develop.
Lesson 5 — Production Rate Limiting: Architecture, Fairness & Failure
Now we move from “how does the algorithm work?” to the question that matters in a real production system:
How do I design a rate limiter that remains useful when traffic, users, regions, dependencies, and failures all behave badly?
This is the level where rate limiting becomes distributed-systems engineering.
________________________________________
1. Start with the actual resource
Don't begin with:
"We need 1,000 requests/sec."
Begin with:
What resource am I protecting?
Suppose:
POST /generate-report
One request might consume:
CPU        → 200 ms
DB         → 5 queries
Memory     → 100 MB peak
Kafka      → 3 messages
External API → 1 call
Meanwhile:
GET /health
might consume almost nothing.
So:
1 report request != 1 health request
A sophisticated limiter therefore may assign costs.
health       → 1 token
profile      → 2 tokens
search       → 5 tokens
report       → 20 tokens
Now you're limiting resource consumption, not merely HTTP request count.
That is a much stronger design.
________________________________________
2. A production system usually has multiple limits
A common mistake is designing one giant limiter:
1000 requests/sec
Real systems often have several independent protection boundaries.
For example:
                    Incoming Request
                           |
                           v
                    Global Limiter
                    100k req/sec
                           |
                           v
                     Per-IP Limit
                      100 req/sec
                           |
                           v
                   Per-Customer Limit
                     1k req/sec
                           |
                           v
                    Endpoint Limit
                 /generate-report
                       10/sec
                           |
                           v
                  Downstream Limit
                  Payment API 100/sec
Each limiter answers a different question.
________________________________________
3. Why global + per-user limits?
Imagine:
Global capacity = 10,000 req/sec
You have:
Customer A → 9,900 req/sec
Customer B →    50 req/sec
Customer C →    50 req/sec
Technically:
9,900 + 50 + 50 = 10,000
No global limit was violated.
But Customer A is effectively monopolizing the system.
That's the noisy-neighbor problem.
So you might define:
Global:
10,000/sec

Per customer:
500/sec
Now:
Customer A → 500/sec
Customer B → 50/sec
Customer C → 50/sec
There is still unused global capacity, but no single customer can destroy everyone else's experience.
This is where fairness enters.
________________________________________
4. Fairness is not the same as equality
Suppose you have customers:
Free customer
Premium customer
Enterprise customer
Giving everyone:
100 req/sec
is equal.
But it may not be fair according to your business model.
You could instead define:
Free       → 10/sec
Premium    → 100/sec
Enterprise → 1000/sec
Now fairness means:
capacity is allocated according to an explicit policy.
So when designing a rate limiter, ask:
Fair to whom?
Possible dimensions:
•	IP 
•	user 
•	API key 
•	customer 
•	tenant 
•	subscription tier 
•	endpoint 
•	region 
________________________________________
5. Hierarchical rate limiting
This leads to an important architecture.
Suppose:
Global = 100,000 req/sec
Then:
Tenant A = 10,000
Tenant B = 5,000
Tenant C = 1,000
And inside Tenant A:
/search = 5,000
/report = 500
/write = 2,000
You can visualize:
Global
  |
  +--------------------------+
  |                          |
Tenant A                  Tenant B
  |                          |
  +----+----+                 |
  |    |    |                 |
search report write         endpoints
This is hierarchical rate limiting.
The request may have to pass multiple policies.
________________________________________
6. But don't overdo hierarchical limits
Every limiter introduces:
latency
state
coordination
failure modes
operational complexity
Imagine:
Request
  |
  v
Global Redis
  |
  v
Customer Redis
  |
  v
Endpoint Redis
  |
  v
Downstream Redis
  |
  v
Application
You've created four additional distributed operations.
That's terrible if every request has to synchronously execute all of them.
So a good design asks:
Which limit actually protects which bottleneck?
Only enforce limits where they provide meaningful protection.
________________________________________
7. Rate limiting vs load shedding
These concepts are related but different.
Rate limiting
Controls:
How much traffic is admitted.
Load shedding
Controls:
What work do we sacrifice when the system is already overloaded?
Imagine:
CPU = 95%
DB connections = 100%
queue = full
Even requests below their nominal rate limit may need to be rejected.
That's load shedding.
For example:
Normal:
accept everything within rate limits

Overloaded:
reject low-priority requests
preserve critical traffic
________________________________________
8. Priority-based load shedding
Suppose your system has:
Payment
Search
Recommendations
Analytics
When overloaded, you might want:
Payment       → preserve
Search        → preserve partially
Recommendations → reduce
Analytics     → reject
This is more sophisticated than a simple:
HTTP 429
You are effectively saying:
Not all work has equal value.
This is an important production principle.
________________________________________
9. Rate limiting + bulkhead
You've already learned Bulkhead, so connect the two.
Suppose:
Rate:
1000 requests/sec
But each request takes:
5 seconds
Potential concurrency:
1000 × 5
= 5000 concurrent requests
That can destroy your thread pool.
So:
Rate Limiter
    ↓
1000 req/sec

Bulkhead
    ↓
200 concurrent requests
Now the system has two protection dimensions:
Rate → how quickly work enters
Concurrency → how much work exists simultaneously
This is why rate limiting does not replace bulkheading.
________________________________________
10. Rate limiting + timeout
Suppose downstream latency increases:
100 ms
  ↓
500 ms
  ↓
2 sec
  ↓
10 sec
Without timeout:
requests accumulate
      ↓
threads occupied
      ↓
connection pool exhausted
      ↓
latency explosion
Rate limiting only controls arrival rate.
It doesn't force an individual request to finish.
Therefore:
Rate Limiter
     ↓
Bulkhead
     ↓
Timeout
can work together.
________________________________________
11. Rate limiting + retry
This is one of the most dangerous interactions.
Suppose:
Rate limit = 100/sec
1000 clients receive:
429
Every client retries after exactly 1 second.
At:
t = 1 sec
you get another synchronized burst.
This is a retry storm.
Therefore rate-limited clients should generally use:
backoff
+
jitter
rather than synchronized retries.
The resilience mechanisms you've studied are not isolated topics—they form a system.
________________________________________
12. Failure mode #1 — Redis is down
This is the classic production question.
Architecture:
Java
 |
 v
Redis
 X
Now what?
There are three broad strategies.
________________________________________
Strategy A — Fail open
Redis failure
     ↓
ALLOW
Good for:
•	high availability 
•	low-risk reads 
•	non-critical APIs 
Bad for:
•	abuse protection 
•	expensive operations 
•	fragile downstreams 
________________________________________
Strategy B — Fail closed
Redis failure
     ↓
REJECT
Good when:
•	protecting a very expensive resource 
•	security is more important than availability 
•	downstream capacity is dangerous to exceed 
But it creates a new problem:
Redis has effectively become part of your application's availability path.
________________________________________
Strategy C — Local fallback
                 Redis
                /     \
          available   unavailable
              |           |
              v           v
          global       local
          limiter      limiter
This is often a practical compromise.
But local fallback cannot perfectly enforce a global limit.
________________________________________
13. Why local fallback is approximate
Suppose:
Global limit = 1000/sec
You have:
10 pods
During Redis failure, each pod could enforce:
100/sec
Then theoretically:
10 × 100 = 1000/sec
Sounds perfect.
But pods don't necessarily receive traffic equally.
Suppose:
Pod A → 500 req/sec
Pod B → 50
Pod C → 20
...
Pod A may reject legitimate requests while other pods have unused capacity.
Therefore local fallback trades:
global precision
for:
availability
________________________________________
14. Failure mode #2 — Redis is slow
This is actually more dangerous than an immediate Redis failure.
Suppose normal Redis latency:
1 ms
Then:
10 ms
Then:
100 ms
Your rate limiter is in the request's critical path.
So:
API latency
≈ application latency
+ Redis latency
If Redis becomes slow:
100 ms Redis
+
50 ms application
=
150 ms
And if requests pile up waiting for Redis, you can get:
thread pool exhaustion
connection pool exhaustion
queue growth
So the limiter itself needs a timeout.
________________________________________
15. Never allow the limiter to wait forever
Bad:
request
   ↓
Redis
   ↓
wait...
   ↓
wait...
   ↓
wait...
Better:
request
   ↓
Redis
   ↓
short timeout
   |
   +---- success → decision
   |
   +---- timeout → failure policy
The rate limiter itself is a distributed dependency.
Therefore the resilience patterns you've learned apply to it too.
________________________________________
16. Should you use a circuit breaker around Redis?
Potentially—but carefully.
Suppose Redis is unhealthy.
Without protection:
1M requests/sec
      ↓
1M Redis calls/sec
      ↓
Redis struggling
      ↓
more latency
      ↓
more queued requests
      ↓
worse Redis load
A circuit breaker can stop hammering an unhealthy Redis.
But then you must decide:
circuit open
     ↓
fail open?
fail closed?
local fallback?
So:
Circuit breaker protects you from a failing rate-limiter dependency, but it doesn't magically decide the correct failure policy.
________________________________________
17. Failure mode #3 — Hot key
Suppose one customer:
enterprise-customer-A
generates:
500,000 req/sec
and all requests touch:
rate_limit:customer-A
That key becomes extremely hot.
Even a Redis cluster may have difficulty because one key is associated with one shard.
This is a fundamental scaling issue.
________________________________________
18. The uncomfortable truth about hot keys
You cannot simply distribute one exact counter across 100 Redis nodes without coordination.
If you split:
customer-A
into:
customer-A-1
customer-A-2
...
customer-A-100
you now need to know:
total tokens across all 100
That requires coordination.
So you face:
Exact global limit
       ↕
Distributed coordination
       ↕
Scalability
You generally can't maximize all three simultaneously.
________________________________________
19. One solution: quota partitioning
Suppose:
Customer A:
100,000 req/sec
You can allocate:
Shard 1 → 10,000
Shard 2 → 10,000
...
Shard 10 → 10,000
Now each shard has independent quota.
But this isn't exactly equivalent to a global bucket.
If:
Shard 1 → 0 traffic
Shard 2 → 20,000 traffic
Shard 2 may reject traffic even though global unused capacity exists on Shard 1.
You've exchanged:
perfect utilization
for:
scalability
This can be an excellent trade-off when the traffic volume justifies it.
________________________________________
20. Failure mode #4 — Multi-region
Imagine:
                    Global Users
                         |
              +----------+----------+
              |                     |
             US                    EU
              |                     |
          Redis US               Redis EU
Global limit:
1000 req/sec
If each region independently allows:
1000/sec
you can get:
2000/sec
globally.
________________________________________
21. Three multi-region strategies
Strategy 1 — Global centralized limiter
US ─────┐
EU ─────┼──> Global Redis
APAC ───┘
Pros:
•	accurate global state 
Cons:
•	cross-region latency 
•	global dependency 
•	harder failure handling 
________________________________________
Strategy 2 — Regional quota
Global = 1000

US  = 500
EU  = 300
APAC = 200
Pros:
•	low latency 
•	regional independence 
Cons:
•	wasted capacity if traffic shifts 
________________________________________
Strategy 3 — Approximate global limit
Each region gets some local capacity and occasionally rebalances.
Pros:
•	scalable 
•	highly available 
Cons:
•	temporary global overshoot 
This is often acceptable when:
1000/sec
really means:
"Approximately 1000/sec."
rather than:
"Under no circumstances may request 1001 occur."
________________________________________
22. Exactness is a business requirement
This is a subtle but extremely important system-design lesson.
Ask:
Does the business actually require exact enforcement?
For an API quota:
1 million requests/month
maybe a few extra requests aren't catastrophic.
For:
payment fraud protection
approximation may be unacceptable.
For:
external API hard quota
exceeding the limit may result in provider penalties.
Therefore:
Required precision
       ↓
determines architecture
Don't introduce distributed consensus just because "exact" sounds better.
________________________________________
23. Failure mode #5 — Clock issues
Token Bucket needs time.
Suppose Pod A thinks:
10:00:00
Pod B thinks:
09:59:58
Now refill calculations can disagree.
For a centralized Redis limiter, one useful approach is to have the Redis-side operation use a consistent server-side time source.
Conceptually:
Java
  |
  v
Redis atomic operation
  |
  +--> current server time
  +--> refill
  +--> consume
This avoids having every application instance independently decide the authoritative timestamp.
________________________________________
24. Fairness problem: bursty customers
Suppose:
Customer A:
100 req/sec

Customer B:
100 req/sec
Both have the same rate.
But A's traffic is:
10, 10, 10, 10...
while B sends:
100, 0, 100, 0...
With Token Bucket, B may be perfectly valid if its bucket capacity allows the burst.
So fairness isn't just about average rate.
There are multiple fairness dimensions:
average rate
burst capacity
concurrency
priority
latency
You need to decide which one matters.
________________________________________
25. Fairness and Weighted Tokens
Suppose:
Free tier:
cost multiplier = 1

Premium:
cost multiplier = 0.5

Enterprise:
cost multiplier = 0.1
Or more intuitively:
Free:
10 tokens/sec

Premium:
100 tokens/sec

Enterprise:
1000 tokens/sec
You can encode business priority directly into token allocation.
But be careful:
Rate limiting is infrastructure policy, not business authorization.
The system should determine the customer's tier from trusted identity/authorization information, not from an arbitrary request parameter.
________________________________________
26. Rate limiting by IP has a weakness
Suppose you use:
10 requests/sec/IP
This can be unfair behind NAT.
For example:
University
    |
    +---- 5,000 users
    |
    v
one public IP
Your limiter sees:
1 IP
but actually represents:
5,000 users
Therefore IP-based limits are useful for abuse protection, but they aren't necessarily a good fairness mechanism.
User/API-key/tenant identity is often better when available.
________________________________________
27. Rate limiting by user also has a weakness
A malicious actor can create:
10,000 accounts
and get:
10,000 × per-user quota
Therefore anti-abuse systems often combine:
IP
+
account
+
device/fingerprint where appropriate
+
API key
+
behavioral signals
Again, rate limiting is only one layer of abuse prevention.
________________________________________
28. Observability is part of the design
You need to know:
How many requests are being rejected?
Who is being rejected?
Why?
Which limiter rejected them?
Is Redis healthy?
Are we hitting capacity?
Useful metrics:
rate_limit_allowed_total
rate_limit_rejected_total
rate_limit_decision_latency
redis_latency
redis_errors
429_total
And dimensions such as:
service
endpoint
region
tenant tier
Be careful with unbounded labels such as raw user IDs—they can destroy metric systems through high cardinality.
That's another production-level consideration.
________________________________________
29. Logging every rejection is usually wrong
Suppose:
100,000 requests/sec
are rejected.
If every rejection creates a log line:
100k logs/sec
your logging infrastructure becomes another bottleneck.
Prefer:
metrics
sampling
aggregated logs
for high-volume limiter events.
For example:
/expensive-api
429 rate = 34%
is much more useful than millions of individual log entries.
