36. A complete senior-level design example
Suppose you're asked:
Design an API serving 1 million users. Each user can make 100 requests/sec. The service has 20 application pods and Redis Cluster. The API must tolerate Redis failure.
A reasonable answer:
Step 1 — Identify limits
Per user:
100 req/sec

Global:
50,000 req/sec

Expensive endpoint:
1000 req/sec
Step 2 — Algorithm
Use:
Token Bucket
because we want:
controlled bursts
+
sustained rate
Step 3 — Shared state
Use Redis for distributed per-user state.
Step 4 — Atomicity
Use an atomic Redis-side operation for:
refill
check
consume
update
Step 5 — Local protection
Use a cheap local limiter to reject obvious overload and reduce Redis traffic.
Step 6 — Redis failure
For normal reads:
local fallback / fail-open
For expensive operations:
fail-closed or strict fallback
depending on business requirements.
Step 7 — Fairness
Per-user limits prevent one customer from monopolizing global capacity.
Step 8 — Observability
Monitor:
429 rate
Redis latency
Redis errors
hot keys
per-tenant rejection
global capacity
Step 9 — Retry behavior
Return:
429
Retry-After
and require clients to use:
backoff + jitter
That's a complete architecture rather than merely naming Redis.
