1. First define the contract
Before writing Java, define what the limiter actually does.
A useful abstraction is:
public interface RateLimiter {

    RateLimitResult tryAcquire(
        String key,
        int permits
    );
}
And:
public record RateLimitResult(
    boolean allowed,
    long remainingTokens,
    Duration retryAfter
) {}
So the application doesn't care whether the implementation uses:
local memory
Redis
Redis Cluster
another service
It only sees:
tryAcquire(...)
       ↓
RateLimitResult
This is an important design principle:
Separate the rate-limiting policy from the storage mechanism.
________________________________________
2. Why tryAcquire()?
Notice we aren't doing:
acquire();
which might block.
We're doing:
tryAcquire();
Rate limiting is fundamentally an admission decision.
You normally don't want:
request
   ↓
wait for rate-limit capacity
   ↓
maybe eventually execute
That can turn a rate limiter into a queue.
Instead:
request
   ↓
check capacity
   ├── allow
   └── reject immediately
This is especially important for protecting overloaded systems.
________________________________________
3. Token Bucket state
Our bucket needs:
final class BucketState {

    double tokens;
    long lastRefillNanos;

    BucketState(double tokens, long lastRefillNanos) {
        this.tokens = tokens;
        this.lastRefillNanos = lastRefillNanos;
    }
}
Conceptually:
Bucket
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ capacity = 100              │
│                             │
│ tokens = 73                 │
│                             │
│ lastRefill = T              │
└─────────────────────────────┘
Configuration:
public record RateLimitConfig(
    long capacity,
    double refillTokensPerSecond
) {}
Example:
new RateLimitConfig(
    100,
    10
);
means:
maximum burst = 100
sustainable rate = 10/sec
