7. The subtle concurrency problem
Suppose:
BucketState state = buckets.get(key);
Two threads can execute:
Thread A → read tokens = 1
Thread B → read tokens = 1
Both consume.
So we need the entire state transition to be atomic.
One simple approach for an in-memory implementation is:
buckets.compute(key, (k, state) -> {
    // refill
    // check
    // consume
    // update
    return state;
});
But there is another challenge:
We need to return the decision from inside the atomic operation.
You can use a holder:
AtomicReference<RateLimitResult> result =
        new AtomicReference<>();

buckets.compute(key, (k, state) -> {

    // calculate

    result.set(...);

    return updatedState;
});

return result.get();
This works, but don't rush into clever code.
For production code, clarity is more important than squeezing everything into one expression.
________________________________________
8. A cleaner local implementation
We can make the bucket itself responsible for its state transition:
final class TokenBucket {

    private final long capacity;
    private final double refillRate;

    private double tokens;
    private long lastRefillNanos;

    TokenBucket(
            long capacity,
            double refillRate,
            long now) {

        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.refillRate = refillRate;
        this.tokens = capacity;
        this.lastRefillNanos = now;
    }

    synchronized RateLimitResult tryAcquire(
            int permits,
            long now) {

        refill(now);

        if (tokens >= permits) {

            tokens -= permits;

            return new RateLimitResult(
                    true,
                    (long) tokens,
                    Duration.ZERO
            );
        }

Now the critical transition:
refill
  ↓
check
  ↓
consume
  ↓
return
is protected by the same lock.
________________________________________
9. Why synchronized here?
For a single JVM, the bucket's state is mutable:
tokens
lastRefillNanos
Multiple threads can access it.
So:
synchronized
gives us:
Thread A
   |
   +-- refill
   +-- check
   +-- consume
   +-- update
   |
   unlock
before:
Thread B
can enter.
This is enough for the local implementation.
But now comes the critical distributed-systems question:
What happens when there are 20 JVMs?
________________________________________
10. Why this local implementation isn't globally correct
Architecture:
                 Load Balancer
                /      |      \
               /       |       \
            Pod A    Pod B    Pod C
              |        |        |
           bucket    bucket   bucket
Each bucket is independent.
So:
Pod A → 100
Pod B → 100
Pod C → 100
can become:
300 requests
even if the intended global limit is:
100
Therefore:
The local limiter is a protection layer, not the authoritative global limiter.
________________________________________
11. Production architecture: two levels
This leads to a very useful architecture:
Request
   |
   v
Local limiter
   |
   v
Redis limiter
   |
   v
Application
Why local first?
Because Redis is a distributed dependency.
If a request can be rejected locally, there's no reason to spend:
