22. HTTP layer
A Spring filter is a natural place for request-level rate limiting.
Conceptually:
@Component
public class RateLimitFilter
        extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    private final RateLimiter rateLimiter;

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(
            HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain filterChain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {

        String key = resolveKey(request);

        RateLimitResult result =
                rateLimiter.tryAcquire(key, 1);

        if (!result.allowed()) {

            response.setStatus(429);

            response.setHeader(
                    "Retry-After",
                    String.valueOf(
                        result.retryAfter()
                                .toSeconds()
                    )
            );

            return;
        }

        filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}
This gives us:
HTTP request
    ↓
filter
    ↓
rate limiter
    ↓
429 OR application
________________________________________
23. But don't put business-specific limits only in the filter
A filter knows:
HTTP request
path
method
headers
identity
But it may not know:
report generation cost = 20
payment operation cost = 50
Therefore:
Filter:
coarse request-level limiting

Service:
business/resource-aware limiting
is often a better design.
