31. Test with a fake clock
Our current code uses:
System.nanoTime()
which makes deterministic unit tests annoying.
A better design is to inject a clock abstraction.
For example:
public interface NanoClock {
    long nanoTime();
}
Production:
public final class SystemNanoClock
        implements NanoClock {

    @Override
    public long nanoTime() {
        return System.nanoTime();
    }
}
Test:
public final class FakeNanoClock
        implements NanoClock {

    private long now;

    @Override
    public long nanoTime() {
        return now;
    }

    public void advance(Duration duration) {
        now += duration.toNanos();
    }
}
Now your tests can say:
clock.advance(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
instead of:
Thread.sleep(5000);
That's a huge improvement.
________________________________________
32. Why Thread.sleep() in tests is bad
Imagine:
Thread.sleep(1000);
in 500 tests.
Your test suite becomes:
500 seconds
And timing tests become flaky because of:
OS scheduling
CI load
GC pauses
CPU contention
Injecting time makes rate-limiter tests:
fast
deterministic
repeatable
This is an important production-quality design technique.
